# IntraCore: параметры HAL блока из базы STM32CubeMX (db/mcu/IP/*_Modes.xml).
# Порождено tools/hal_import — руками не править. Формат: src/Docs/Description/HalCatalogFormat.md
[Ip]
Name=LWIP
Version=v2.0.3_Cube

[Parameters]
# имя|подпись|группа|единица|мин|макс|описание
LwIP Version|LwIP Version (Version of LwIP supported by CubeMX ** CubeMX specific **)|LwIP Version||||<font color="blue">LwIP version supported by CubeMX is the <b>O</b>fficial <b>R</b>elease <b>2.0.3</b> dated <b>Sept. 16, 2017.</b></font> MQTT and HOOK configuration is not supported via CubeMx GUI. LwIP stack has an internal parameter defined in init.h providing its version: LWIP_VERSION. Here, the LwIP Version is just displayed to highlight the LwIP version supported by CubeMX. <b>Warning</b>: This parameter has been introduced by CubeMX and is not configurable, LWIP_VERSION being already defined in init.h.
LWIP_DHCP|LWIP_DHCP (DHCP Module)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) module is enabled.
IP_ADDRESS|IP_ADDRESS (IP Address)|IP Address Settings|||255|
NETMASK_ADDRESS|NETMASK_ADDRESS (Netmask Address)|IP Address Settings|||255|
GATEWAY_ADDRESS|GATEWAY_ADDRESS (Gateway Address)|IP Address Settings|||255|
WITH_RTOS|WITH_RTOS (Use FREERTOS ** CubeMX specific **)|RTOS Dependency||||This parameter is enabled as soon as FREERTOS is enabled in MiddleWares tree. <b>Warning</b>: This parameter has been introduced by CubeMX.
WITH_MBEDTLS|WITH_MBEDTLS (Use MBEDTLS ** CubeMX specific **)|MBEDTLS Dependency||||<b>Warning</b>: This parameter has been introduced by CubeMX. This parameter is enabled as soon as MBEDTLS and FREERTOS are enabled in MiddleWares tree. Consequently:<font color="blue"> - MX_LWIP_Init() function is generated within mbedtls_net_init() function in net_sockets.c file - Up to user to call mbedtls_net_init() function in MBEDTLS initialization step - LWIP_DNS value is forced to Enabled - LWIP_UDP value is forced to Enabled - LWIP_USE_EXTERNAL_MBEDTLS value is forced to Enabled - LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_ARC4 value is forced to Disabled (excluding arc4.c/arc4.h files from lwip stack) - LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_DES value is forced to Disabled (excluding des.c/des.h files from lwip stack) - LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_MD4 is forced to Disabled (excluding md4.c/md4.h files from lwip stack) - LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_MD5 is forced to Disabled (excluding md5.c/md5.h files from lwip stack) - LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_SHA1 is forced to Disabled (excluding sha1.c/sha1.h files from lwip stack) - ecp.c/ecp.h ppp files are excluded from lwip stack also.</font>
CMSIS_VERSION|CMSIS_VERSION (CMSIS API Version used)|RTOS Dependency||||This parameter has been introduced by CubeMX and gives CMSIS version used.
RTOS_USE_NEWLIB_REENTRANT|RTOS_USE_NEWLIB_REENTRANT (FREERTOS USE_NEWLIB_REENTRANT 1)|RTOS Dependency||||This parameter is enabled as soon as FREERTOS is enabled in MiddleWares tree. <b>Warning</b>: This parameter has been introduced by CubeMX.
PHY Driver|PHY Driver|Platform Settings||||<font color="red">Platform Settings <b>PHY Driver</b> must be configured from the LwIP '<b>Platform Settings</b>' top right tab.</font> PHY Driver can be set either to Choose or to LAN8742: - <b>Choose</b>: BSP PHY Driver components provided by ST are not used and up to user to implement his own driver with same architecture and constraints than LAN8742 ; ST specification will be published soon to explain how user can develop his own BSP driver component. - <b>LAN8742</b>: LAN8742 BSP PHY Driver component is used and STM32CubeMX copies lan8742.c/.h files from ST Firmware Package to the user's project Drivers/BSP/Components directory.</ul> <i>Note</i>: BSP components files cannot be updated or they will be overwritten at each project generation.
LWIP_ICMP|LWIP_ICMP (ICMP Module Activation)|Protocols Options||||This option enables ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol) module inside the IP stack.Be careful, disable that make your product non-compliant to RFC1122. - "Debug" tab. <b>Dependency</b>: None. <b>Note</b>: For more options on ICMP, please check in: - "Key Options" tab within "ICMP/IGMP Options" - "Statistics" tab - "Checksum" tab
LWIP_IGMP|LWIP_IGMP (IGMP Module)|Protocols Options||||When this parameter is enabled, it turns on IGMP (Internet Group Management Protocol) module. LWIP_RAND() is in cc.h (in \system\arch). <b>Note</b>: For more options on IGMP, please check in: - "Key Options" tab within "ICMP/IGMP", "Internal Memory Pool Sizes" and "LOOPIF" Options - "Statistics" tab - "Debug" tab.
LWIP_DNS|LWIP_DNS (DNS Module)|Protocols Options||||When this parameter is enabled, it turns on DNS (Domain Name Server) module. UDP must be available for DNS transport. <b>Note</b>: For more options on DNS, please check in: - "Key Options" tab within "DNS" and "Internal Memory Pool Sizes" Options - "Debug" tab.
LWIP_UDP|LWIP_UDP (UDP Module)|Protocols Options||||When this parameter is enabled, it turns on UDP (User Datagram Protocol). <b>Note</b>: For more options on UDP, please check in: - "Key Options" tab within "UDP", "IP" and "Thread" Options - "Statistics" tab - "Checksum" tab - "Debug" tab.
MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB|MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB (Number of UDP Connections)|Protocols Options||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & LWIP_UDP, 1, 0)|2147483647|Number of UDP protocol control blocks. One per active UDP connection (requires the LWIP_UDP option enabled). <b>Dependency</b>: Use MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB requires MEMP_MEM_MALLOC disabled and LWIP_UDP enabled, it is mandatory to have MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB>0. <b>Note</b>: For more memory options, see in "Key Options" tab within "Internal Memory Pool Sizes" Options.
LWIP_TCP|LWIP_TCP (TCP Module)|Protocols Options||||When this parameter is enabled, it turns on TCP. <b>Note</b>: For more TCP options, see in: - "Key Options" tab within "TCP", "IP", "Internal Memory Pool Sizes", "Thread" and "Sequential Layer" Options - "Perf/Checks" tab - "Statistics" tab - "Checksum" tab - "Debug" tab.
MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB|MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB (Number of TCP Connections)|Protocols Options||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & LWIP_TCP, 1, 0)|2147483647|Number of simultatenously active TCP connections (requires the LWIP_TCP option enabled). <b>Dependency</b>: Use MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB requires MEMP_MEM_MALLOC disabled and LWIP_TCP enabled, it is mandatory to have MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB>0. <b>Note</b>: For more memory options, see in "Key Options" tab within "Internal Memory Pool Sizes" Options.
heth|heth (Ethernet Handle)|Global||0|1|
gnetif|gnetif (Network Interface)|Global||0|1|
ipaddr|ipaddr (IP Address)|Global||0|1|
netmask|netmask (Netmask IP Address)|Global||0|1|
gw|gw (Gateway Address)|Global||0|1|
PHY|PHY (Phy MII)|RTOS Settings||||PHY MII parameter.
TX_DESCRIPTOR_ADDRESS|TX_DESCRIPTOR_ADDRESS (First Tx Descriptor Address)|RTOS Settings||||First Tx Descriptor Address.
RX_DESCRIPTOR_ADDRESS|RX_DESCRIPTOR_ADDRESS (First Rx Descriptor Address)|RTOS Settings||||First Rx Descriptor Address.
RX_BUFFER_ADDRESS|RX_BUFFER_ADDRESS (Rx Buffer Address)|RTOS Settings||||Rx Buffer Address.
NO_SYS|NO_SYS (OS Awarness)|Infrastructure - OS Awarness Option||||When NO_SYS=0, lwIP is used with selected Operating System (FreeRTOS) and its related thread, semaphores, mutexes or mboxes. This means threaded APIs are used (socket, netconn, i.e. everything in the 'api' folder). <b>Warning</b>: Take care about NO_SYS value: NO_SYS(OS Used)=0.
LWIP_TIMERS|LWIP_TIMERS (Use Support For sys_timeout)|Infrastructure - Timers Options||||Enable this parameter to use support for sys_timeout and lwip-internal cyclic timers (the array of lwip-internal cyclic timers is still provided, check NO_SYS_NO_TIMERS for compatibility to old versions).
LWIP_TIMERS_CUSTOM|LWIP_TIMERS_CUSTOM (Own Timer Implementation)|Infrastructure - Timers Options||||Enable this parameter to provide your own timer implementation. Function prototypes in timeouts.h and the array of lwip-internal cyclic timers are still included, but the implementation is not. The following functions will be required: sys_timeouts_init(), sys_timeout(), sys_untimeout(), sys_timeouts_mbox_fetch().
MEMCPY(dst,src,len)|MEMCPY(dst,src,len) (Normal Memory Copy)|Infrastructure - Memory Copy Options||||This parameter allows to replace the default implementation included in C library by a faster user function. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SMEMCPY(dst,src,len)|SMEMCPY(dst,src,len) (Small Memory Copy)|Infrastructure - Memory Copy Options||||This parameter allows to replace the default implementation by a user hand made. Some compilers (e.g. gcc) can inline a call to memcpy() if the length is known at compile time and is small. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_MPU_COMPATIBLE|LWIP_MPU_COMPATIBLE (Special Memory Management)|Infrastructure - Core Locking and MPU Options||||This parameter allows to enable special memory management mechanism which makes lwip able to work on MPU (Memory Protection Unit) system by not passing stack-pointers to other threads (this decreases performance as memory is allocated from pools instead of keeping it on the stack). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING|LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING (TCPIP Core Locking)|Infrastructure - Core Locking and MPU Options||||Enable this option to create a global mutex that is held during TCPIP thread operations. It can be locked by client code to perform lwIP operations without changing into TCPIP thread using callbacks. See LOCK_TCPIP_CORE() and UNLOCK_TCPIP_CORE(). Your system should provide mutexes supporting priority inversion to use this. <b>Dependency</b>: When using LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING_INPUT, LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING must be enabled, too.
LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING_INPUT|LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING_INPUT (TCPIP Core Locking Input)|Infrastructure - Core Locking and MPU Options||||When LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING is enabled, this lets tcpip_input() grab the mutex for input packets as well, instead of allocating a message and passing it to tcpip_thread. <b>Warning</b>: this does not work when tcpip_input() is called from interrupt context!
SYS_LIGHTWEIGHT_PROT|SYS_LIGHTWEIGHT_PROT (Memory Functions Protection)|Infrastructure - Core Locking and MPU Options||||Enable this option if you want inter-task protection (and task-vs-interrupt protection) for certain critical regions during buffer allocation, deallocation and memory allocation and deallocation. <b>Warning</b>: This is required when using lwIP from more than one context! If you disable this, you must be sure what you are doing!
MEM_LIBC_MALLOC|MEM_LIBC_MALLOC (User Memory Library)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||This option can be set with: - Enable: use malloc/free/realloc provided by your C-library instead of the lwip internal allocator. Can save code size if you already use it. - Disable: use LwIP internal allocator library <b>Dependency</b>: If MEM_LIBC_MALLOC=1, MEMP_USE_POOLS cannot be enabled. MEM_STATS value setting depends on MEM_LIBC_MALLOC value.
MEMP_MEM_MALLOC|MEMP_MEM_MALLOC (User Memory Pool Functions)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||This option is set as followed: - Enabled: Use User C Pool Alloctor Functions i.e mem_malloc/mem_free instead of the lwip pool allocator, especially useful with MEM_LIBC_MALLOC but handle with care regarding execution speed (heap alloc can be much slower than pool alloc) and usage from interrupts (especially if your netif driver allocates PBUF_POOL pbufs for received frames from interrupt!) - Disabled: Use LwIP Internal Pool Allocator Memory Functions. <b>Warning</b>: Currently, this uses the heap for ALL pools (also for private pools, not only for internal pools defined in memp_std.h)! <b>Dependency</b>: There are several other parameters linked to this one: MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB, MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB, MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB, MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG, MEMP_NUM_REASSDATA, MEMP_NUM_IGMP_GROUP, MEMP_NUM_SYS_TIMEOUT, MEMP_NUM_NETCONN, MEMP_NUM_TCPIP_MSG_API, ARP_QUEUEING, IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS, LWIP_RAW, LWIP_IGMP, LWIP_UDP, LWIP_TCP, TCP_WND, TCP_SND_QUEUELEN, LWIP_NETCONN, LWIP_SOCKET. <b>Dependency</b>:MEMP_MEM_MALLOC and MEM_USE_POOLS may not both be simultaneously enabled
MEM_ALIGNMENT|MEM_ALIGNMENT (Memory Byte Alignment of CPU)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options|Byte(s)|||This parameter should be set to the alignment of the CPU: 4 byte alignment or 2 byte alignment. <b>Dependency</b>: PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE must be greater than MEM_ALIGNMENT.
MEM_SIZE|MEM_SIZE (Heap Memory Size)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Size of the heap memory. If the application will send a lot of data that needs to be copied, this should be set high. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_OVERFLOW_CHECK|MEMP_OVERFLOW_CHECK (Memory Pool Overflow Protection)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||0|2147483647|The memp overflow protection parameter reserves a configurable amount of bytes before and after each memp element in every pool and fills it with a prominent default value: - MEMP_OVERFLOW_CHECK=0: no checking - MEMP_OVERFLOW_CHECK=1: checks each element when it is freed - MEMP_OVERFLOW_CHECK>1: checks each element in every pool every time. The function memp_malloc() or memp_free() is called (useful but slow!).
MEMP_SANITY_CHECK|MEMP_SANITY_CHECK (Memory Pool Sanity Check)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||If this option is enabled, sanity check is run after each memp_free() to make sure that there are no cycles in the linked lists. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEM_USE_POOLS|MEM_USE_POOLS (Use an Alternative to malloc Function)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||If this option is enabled, an alternative to malloc() is used by allocating from a set of memory pools of various sizes. When mem_malloc is called, an element of the smallest pool that can provide the length needed is returned. To use this, MEMP_USE_CUSTOM_POOLS also has to be enabled.
MEM_USE_POOLS_TRY_BIGGER_POOL|MEM_USE_POOLS_TRY_BIGGER_POOL (Try Next Bigger Pool if Empty Malloc Pool)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||When this option is enabled, if one malloc-pool is empty, the next bigger pool is tried. <b>Warning</b>: Enabling this parameter might waste memory but it can make a system more reliable. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_USE_CUSTOM_POOLS|MEMP_USE_CUSTOM_POOLS (Define Additional Pools)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||Enable this options to include a user file lwippools.h that defines additional pools beyond the standard ones required by lwIP. <b>Warning</b>: If you set this to 1, you must have lwippools.h in your include path somewhere and you must define your pools within lwippools.h. <b>Dependency</b>: If MEMP_USE_CUSTOM_POOLS=0, MEM_USE_POOLS will be disabled and if MEMP_USE_CUSTOM_POOLS=1, MEM_USE_POOLS can be enabled.
LWIP_ALLOW_MEM_FREE_FROM_OTHER_CONTEXT|LWIP_ALLOW_MEM_FREE_FROM_OTHER_CONTEXT (Allow Memory Free From Other Context)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||||Enable this option if you want to free PBUF_RAM pbufs (or call mem_free()) from interrupt context (or another context that doesn't allow waiting for a semaphore). If set to 1, mem_malloc will be protected by a semaphore and SYS_ARCH_PROTECT, while mem_free will only use SYS_ARCH_PROTECT. mem_malloc SYS_ARCH_UNPROTECTs with each loop so that mem_free can run. <b>Warning</b>: As you can see from the above description, this leads to dis-/ enabling interrupts often, which can be slow! Also, on low memory, mem_malloc can need longer. If you don't want that, at least for NO_SYS=0, you can still use the following functions to enqueue a deallocation call which then runs in the tcpip_thread context: - pbuf_free_callback(p) - mem_free_callback(m);. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_RAM_HEAP_POINTER|LWIP_RAM_HEAP_POINTER (RAM Heap Pointer)|Infrastructure - Heap and Memory Pools Options||0x00000000|0xFFFFFFFF|If you want to relocate the heap to external memory, simply set a void-pointer to that location and make sure the memory at that location is big enough.
MEMP_NUM_PBUF|MEMP_NUM_PBUF (Number of Memory Pool struct Pbufs)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of memp struct pbufs (used for PBUF_ROM and PBUF_REF). If the application sends a lot of data out of ROM (or other static memory), this should be set high. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB|MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB (Number of Raw Protocol Control Blocks)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & LWIP_RAW, 1, 0)|2147483647|Number of Raw connection PCBs (requires the LWIP_RAW option). <b>Dependency</b>: Use MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB requires MEMP_MEM_MALLOC=0 and if LWIP_RAW=1, it is mandatory to have MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB>0.
MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB_LISTEN|MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB_LISTEN (Number of Listening TCP Connections)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of listening TCP connections (requires the LWIP_TCP option). This corresponds to the maximum number of TCP ports which may be simultaneously listened on. <b>Dependency</b>: MEMP_NUM_NETCONN is link to MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB_LISTEN value.
MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG|MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG (Number of TCP Segments simultaneously queued)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(!LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_TCP & !MEMP_MEM_MALLOC, TCP_SND_QUEUELEN, 0)|2147483647|Number of simultaneously queued TCP segments (requires the LWIP_TCP option). <b>Dependency</b>: MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG should be at least as big as TCP_SND_QUEUELEN when LWIP_TCP and LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS are enabled and MEMP_MEM_MALLOC disabled. If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1 to avoid Min value limitation.
MEMP_NUM_REASSDATA|MEMP_NUM_REASSDATA (Number of IP packets simultaneously queued for reassembly)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & IP_REASSEMBLY, IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS, 1000000)|Number of IP packets simultaneously queued for reassembly (whole packets, not fragments!). <b>Dependency</b>: MEMP_NUM_REASSDATA must be less than IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS when IP_REASSEMBLY is enabled and MEMP_MEM_MALLOC disabled.
MEMP_NUM_FRAG_PBUF|MEMP_NUM_FRAG_PBUF (Number of IP Fragments simultaneously sent)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of IP fragments simultaneously sent (fragments, not whole packets!). This is only used with LWIP_NETIF_TX_SINGLE_PBUF==0 and only has to be > 1 with DMA-enabled MACs where the packet is not yet sent when netif->output returns. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_NUM_ARP_QUEUE|MEMP_NUM_ARP_QUEUE (Number of simulateously queued Pbufs waiting for an ARP Request)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & LWIP_ARP & ARP_QUEUEING, 1, 0)|2147483647|Number of simulateously queued outgoing packets (pbufs) that are waiting for an ARP request (to resolve their destination address) to finish (requires the ARP_QUEUEING option). <b>Dependency</b>: MEMP_NUM_ARP_QUEUE must be > 1 to use ARP_QUEUEING when LWIP_ARP is enabled and MEMP_MEM_MALLOC disabled.
MEMP_NUM_IGMP_GROUP|MEMP_NUM_IGMP_GROUP (Number of Multicast Groups)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & LWIP_IGMP,2,0)|2147483647|Number of multicast groups whose network interfaces can be members at the same time: one per netif (allsystems group) plus one per netif membership. It requires the LWIP_IGMP option enabled. <b>Dependency</b>: MEMP_NUM_IGMP_GROUP must be greater than 1 if LWIP_IGMP option is enabled when MEMP_MEM_MALLOC is disabled. <b>Recommendation</b>: This value must be at least twice the number of active network interfaces active in the configuration.
MEMP_NUM_SYS_TIMEOUT|MEMP_NUM_SYS_TIMEOUT (Number of Timeouts simultateously active)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & LWIP_TIMERS, LWIP_TCP+IP_REASSEMBLY+LWIP_ARP+(2*LWIP_DHCP)+LWIP_AUTOIP+LWIP_IGMP+LWIP_DNS+PPP_SUPPORT+IF(LWIP_IPV6,1+LWIP_IPV6_REASS+LWIP_IPV6_MLD,0), 0)|2147483647|Number of simulateously active timeouts. The default number of timeouts is calculated here for all enabled modules. <b>Dependency</b>: If MEMP_MEM_MALLOC and LWIP_TIMERS are enabled, the MEMP_NUM_SYS_TIMEOUT must have as minimum value: LWIP_TCP + IP_REASSEMBLY + LWIP_ARP + (2*LWIP_DHCP) + LWIP_AUTOIP + LWIP_IGMP + LWIP_DNS + PPP_SUPPORT*6*MEMP_NUM_PPP_PCB + (LWIP_IPV6? (1+LWIP_IPV6_REASS+LWIP_IPV6_MLD) : 0) (value expected for each parameter is either '0' or '1').
MEMP_NUM_NETBUF|MEMP_NUM_NETBUF (Number of Netbufs Structures)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of netbuf structures which may be in use simultaneously (only needed if you use the sequential API like LWIP_NETCONN or LWIP_SOCKET). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_NUM_NETCONN|MEMP_NUM_NETCONN (Number of Netconns Structures)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & !LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_NETCONN & LWIP_SOCKET, 1, 0)|=IF(MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & !LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS & !LWIP_NETCONN & !LWIP_SOCKET, MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB + MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB_LISTEN + MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB + MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB, 2147483647)|Number of struct netconns (only needed if you use the sequential API, like like LWIP_NETCONN or LWIP_SOCKET). <b>Dependency</b>: There are 2 dependencies: 1) When LWIP_NETCONN is enabled, MEMP_MEM_MALLOC and LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS are disabled, MEMP_NUM_NETCONN must not ecceed MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB + MEMP_NUM_TCP_PCB_LISTEN + MEMP_NUM_UDP_PCB + MEMP_NUM_RAW_PCB 2) When LWIP_NETCONN, LWIP_SOCKET and MEMP_MEM_MALLOC are all enabled with LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS disabled, MEMP_NUM_NETCONN cannot be 0. If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1.
MEMP_NUM_TCPIP_MSG_API|MEMP_NUM_TCPIP_MSG_API (Number of TCPIP Message Structures Used for Callback)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||=IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & (LWIP_NETCONN / LWIP_SOCKET), 1, 0)|2147483647|Number of struct tcpip_msg, which are used for callback/timeout API communication (only needed if you use tcpip.c). <b>Dependency</b>: Use MEMP_NUM_TCPIP_MSG_API requires MEMP_MEM_MALLOC disabled and LWIP_NETCONN or LWIP_SOCKET enabled.
MEMP_NUM_TCPIP_MSG_INPKT|MEMP_NUM_TCPIP_MSG_INPKT (Number of TCPIP Message Structures Used for Incoming Packets)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of struct tcpip_msg used for incoming packets (only needed if you use tcpip.c). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_NUM_NETDB|MEMP_NUM_NETDB (Number of Concurrent lwip_addrinfo() Calls)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently running lwip_addrinfo() calls (before freeing the corresponding memory using lwip_freeaddrinfo()). <b>Dependency</b>: Used if LWIP_DNS is enabled.
MEMP_NUM_LOCALHOSTLIST|MEMP_NUM_LOCALHOSTLIST (Number of Host Entries in the Local Host List)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of host entries in the local host list if DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_IS_DYNAMIC=1. <b>Dependency</b>: Used if LWIP_DNS is enabled.
MEMP_NUM_API_MSG|MEMP_NUM_API_MSG (Number of Concurrent Active API Calls)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active calls to various socket, netconn, and tcpip functions.
MEMP_NUM_DNS_API_MSG|MEMP_NUM_DNS_API_MSG (Number of Concurrent Active DNS Calls)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active calls to netconn_gethostbyname.
MEMP_NUM_SOCKET_SETGETSOCKOPT_DATA|MEMP_NUM_SOCKET_SETGETSOCKOPT_DATA (Number of Concurrent Active Socket Calls)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active calls to getsockopt/setsockopt.
MEMP_NUM_NETIFAPI_MSG|MEMP_NUM_NETIFAPI_MSG (Number of Concurrent Active Netif Calls)|Infrastructure - Internal Memory Pool Sizes||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active calls to netifapi functions.
LWIP_SUPPORT_CUSTOM_PBUF|LWIP_SUPPORT_CUSTOM_PBUF (Custom Pbuf)|Pbuf Options||||Custom Pbuf parameter.
PBUF_POOL_SIZE|PBUF_POOL_SIZE (Number of Buffers in the Pbuf Pool)|Pbuf Options||=IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS+1|2147483647|Number of buffers in the pbuf pool.
PBUF_LINK_HLEN|PBUF_LINK_HLEN (Number of Bytes for Link Level Header)|Pbuf Options|Byte(s)|14|=PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE-PBUF_IP_HLEN-PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN-1|Number of bytes that should be allocated for a link level header. The default is 14, the standard value for Ethernet. <b>Warning</b>: To handle corner cases, PBUF_LINK_HLEN must be less than PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE - 40 (IP_HLEN + PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN) and greater or equal than 14.
PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN|PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN (Number of Bytes for Link Encapsulation Level Header)|Pbuf Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|the number of bytes that should be allocated for an additional encapsulation header before ethernet headers (e.g. 802.11). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE|PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE (Size of each pbuf in the pbuf pool)|Pbuf Options|Byte(s)|=MAX(IF(!MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & PBUF_POOL_SIZE & LWIP_TCP & !LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS, PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN + PBUF_LINK_HLEN + PBUF_IP_HLEN + PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN + 1,0),MEM_ALIGNMENT)|2147483647|Size of each pbuf in the pbuf pool. The default is designed to accommodate single full size TCP frame in one pbuf, including TCP_MSS, IP header, and link header: PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE default value is LWIP_MEM_ALIGN_SIZE(TCP_MSS+40+PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN+PBUF_LINK_HLEN) <b>Note</b>: LWIP_MEM_ALIGN_SIZE(size) equals to: (size+MEM_ALIGNMENT-1U) & ~(MEM_ALIGNMENT-1U). <b>Warning</b>: PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE should be greater or equal than PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN + PBUF_LINK_HLEN + PBUF_IP_HLEN + PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN if MEMP_MEM_MALLOC and LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS are disabled and if LWIP_TCP and PBUF_POOL_SIZE are enabled, MEM_ALIGNMENT otherwise. If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1 to keep only limitation on MEM_ALIGNMENT.
PBUF_IP_HLEN|PBUF_IP_HLEN (PBUF_IP_HLEN)|Pbuf Options||0|65535|
PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN|PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN (PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN)|Pbuf Options||0|65535|
LWIP_ARP|LWIP_ARP (ARP Functionality)|IPv4 - ARP Options||||Enable ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) functionality. When ARP is disabled, either PPP or SLIPIF should be enabled or user protocol interface should be added.
ARP_TABLE_SIZE|ARP_TABLE_SIZE (Number of Active MAC-IP Address Pairs Cached)|IPv4 - ARP Options||0|127|Number of active MAC-IP address pairs cached. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ARP_MAXAGE|ARP_MAXAGE (Time for Valid ARP Entry)|IPv4 - ARP Options||0|2147483647|The time an ARP entry stays valid after its last update, for ARP_TMR_INTERVAL = 1000, this is (60 * 5) seconds = 5 minutes. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ARP_QUEUEING|ARP_QUEUEING (Multiple Outgoing Queued Packets)|IPv4 - ARP Options||||Multiple outgoing packets are queued during hardware address resolution. By default, only the most recent packet is queued per IP address. This is sufficient for most protocols and mainly reduces TCP connection startup time. Set this to 1 if you know your application sends more than one packet in a row to an IP address that is not in the ARP cache.
ARP_QUEUE_LEN|ARP_QUEUE_LEN (Max ARP Queue Length)|IPv4 - ARP Options||0|2147483647|The maximum number of packets which may be queued for each unresolved address by other network layers. Defaults to 3, 0 means disabled. Old packets are dropped, new packets are queued. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ETHARP_SUPPORT_VLAN|ETHARP_SUPPORT_VLAN (Receiving Ethernet Packets with VLAN Header)|IPv4 - ARP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, receiving and sending ethernet packets with VLAN header is supported. See the description of LWIP_HOOK_VLAN_CHECK and LWIP_HOOK_VLAN_SET hooks to check/set VLAN headers. Additionally, you can define ETHARP_VLAN_CHECK to an u16_t VLAN ID to check. If ETHARP_VLAN_CHECK is defined, only VLAN-traffic for this VLAN is accepted. If ETHARP_VLAN_CHECK is not defined, all traffic is accepted. Alternatively, define a function/define ETHARP_VLAN_CHECK_FN(eth_hdr, vlan) that returns 1 to accept a packet or 0 to drop a packet. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ETHERNET|LWIP_ETHERNET (Ethernet Module)|IPv4 - ARP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, ethernet is supported even though ARP might be disabled. <b>Warning</b>: Ethernet needs to be kept activated to enable LwIP in STM32CubeMX. <b>Dependency</b>: LWIP_ETHERNET needs to be turned on for LWIP_ARP.
ETH_PAD_SIZE|ETH_PAD_SIZE (Bytes Added before Ethernet Header)|IPv4 - ARP Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Number of bytes added before the ethernet header to ensure alignment of payload after that header. Since the header is 14 bytes long, without this padding e.g. addresses in the IP header will not be aligned on a 32-bit boundary, so setting this to 2 can speed up 32-bit-platforms. <b>Dependency</b>: By default PBUF_LINK_HLEN value is set to ETH_PAD_SIZE+14.
ETHARP_SUPPORT_STATIC_ENTRIES|ETHARP_SUPPORT_STATIC_ENTRIES (Support Static ARP Table Entries)|IPv4 - ARP Options||||This parameter enables code to support static ARP table entries (using etharp_add_static_entry/etharp_remove_static_entry). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ETHARP_TABLE_MATCH_NETIF|ETHARP_TABLE_MATCH_NETIF (Support Netif for ARP Table)|IPv4 - ARP Options||||Match netif for ARP table entries. If disabled, duplicate IP address on multiple netifs are not supported (but this should only occur for AutoIP). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV4|LWIP_IPV4 (Enable IPv4)|IPv4 Options||||This parameter Enables IPv4 protocol. <b>Dependency</b>: This parameter has dependencies with IP_FORWARD, IP_REASSEMBLY, IP_FRAG, LWIP_DHCP, LWIP_AUTOIP, LWIP_IGMP and LWIP_MULTICAST_TX_OPTIONS.
IP_FORWARD|IP_FORWARD (Forward IP Packets Across Network Interfaces)|IPv4 Options||||This parameter enables the ability to forward IP packets across network interfaces. If you are going to run lwIP on a device with only one network interface, define this to 0.
IP_OPTIONS_ALLOWED|IP_OPTIONS_ALLOWED (IP Options Allowed)|IPv4 Options||||This option defines the behavior for IP options: - IP_OPTIONS_ALLOWED=0: All packets with IP options are dropped. - IP_OPTIONS_ALLOWED=1: IP options are allowed (but not parsed). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
IP_REASSEMBLY|IP_REASSEMBLY (Incoming Fragmented IP packets Reassembly)|IPv4 Options||||When this parameter is enabled, incoming fragmented IP packets are reassembled. Note that this option does not affect outgoing packet sizes, which can be controlled via IP_FRAG.
IP_FRAG|IP_FRAG (IP Packets Fragmentation)|IPv4 Options||||When this parameter is enabled, outgoing IP packets are fragmented if their size exceeds MTU. Note that this option does not affect incoming packet sizes, which can be controlled via IP_REASSEMBLY.
IP_REASS_MAXAGE|IP_REASS_MAXAGE (IP Packets Fragmentation if Size exceeds MTU)|IPv4 Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|Maximum time (in multiples of IP_TMR_INTERVAL - so seconds, normally) a fragmented IP packet waits for all fragments to arrive. If not all fragments arrived in this time, the whole packet is discarded. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS|IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS (Maximum of Pbufs waiting to Be Reassembled)|IPv4 Options||=IF(MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & IP_REASSEMBLY, MEMP_NUM_REASSDATA, 0)|=PBUF_POOL_SIZE-1|Total maximum amount of pbufs waiting to be reassembled. Since the received pbufs are enqueued, be sure to configure PBUF_POOL_SIZE > IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS so that the stack is still able to receive packets even if the maximum amount of fragments is enqueued for reassembly!. <b>Dependency</b>: Use IP_REASSEMBLY requires IP_REASS_MAX_PBUFS>=MEMP_NUM_REASSDATA when MEMP_MEM_MALLOC is enabled.
IP_DEFAULT_TTL|IP_DEFAULT_TTL (Time-To-Live Used by Transport Layers)|IPv4 Options|Node(s)|0|255|Default value for Time-To-Live used by transport layers. This tells how long the packets may live on the network before they are dropped. Each time the packet passes a router, firewall, computer, i.e a node, the TTL is decremented with one step. Setting the TTL to 255 would be considered rude since this would make a packet live an extremely long time on the internet. <b>Dependency</b>: IP_DEFAULT_TTL is used to set by default: ICMP_TTL, RAW_TTL, UDP_TTL and TCP_TTL.
IP_SOF_BROADCAST|IP_SOF_BROADCAST (Broadcast Filter per Pcb on UDP and Raw Send Operations)|IPv4 Options||||When this parameter is enabled, the SOF_BROADCAST field is used to enable broadcast filter per pcb on udp and raw send operations. To enable broadcast filter on recv operations, you also have to set IP_SOF_BROADCAST_RECV=1. <b>Dependency</b>: Use IP_SOF_BROADCAST_RECV requires IP_SOF_BROADCAST enabled.
IP_SOF_BROADCAST_RECV|IP_SOF_BROADCAST_RECV (Broadcast Filter on recv Operations)|IPv4 Options||||When this parameter is enabled, broadcast filter on recv operations is enabled.
IP_FORWARD_ALLOW_TX_ON_RX_NETIF|IP_FORWARD_ALLOW_TX_ON_RX_NETIF (IP Forward to send Packets back out on the NETIF)|IPv4 Options||||Set this parameter allows ip_forward() to send packets back out on the netif where it was received. This should only be used for wireless networks. <b>Warning</b>: When this is 1, make sure your netif driver correctly marks incoming link-layer-broadcast/multicast packets as such using the corresponding pbuf flags! <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_RANDOMIZE_INITIAL_LOCAL_PORTS|LWIP_RANDOMIZE_INITIAL_LOCAL_PORTS (Randomizes Local Port for the First Local TCP/UDP PCB)|IPv4 Options||||Set this parameter randomizes the local port for the first local TCP/UDP pcb (default=0). This can prevent creating predictable port numbers after booting a device. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ICMP_TTL|ICMP_TTL (Time-To-Live Used by ICMP Packets)|IPv4 - ICMP Options|Node(s)|0|255|Default value for Time-To-Live used by ICMP packets. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_BROADCAST_PING|LWIP_BROADCAST_PING (Respond to Broadcast Pings)|IPv4 - ICMP Options||||Set this parameter allows responding to broadcast pings (default is unicast only). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_MULTICAST_PING|LWIP_MULTICAST_PING (Respond to Multicast Pings)|IPv4 - ICMP Options||||Set this parameter to allow responding to multicast pings (default is unicast only). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_MULTICAST_TX_OPTIONS|LWIP_MULTICAST_TX_OPTIONS (Multicast TX Support)|IPv4 - Multicast Option||||Set this parameter to allow multicast TX support like the socket options IP_MULTICAST_TTL/IP_MULTICAST_IF/IP_MULTICAST_LOOP.
LWIP_RAW|LWIP_RAW (Use Raw LwIP API)|Callback - Raw Options||||If this parameter is enabled, the application layer to hook into the IP layer itself is enabled.
RAW_TTL|RAW_TTL (Time-To-Live Used by Raw Packets)|Callback - Raw Options|Node(s)|0|255|Default value for Time-To-Live used by Raw packets. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DHCP_DOES_ARP_CHECK|DHCP_DOES_ARP_CHECK (DHCP with ARP Check on Offered Address)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, an ARP check on the offered address is done.
LWIP_DHCP_CHECK_LINK_UP|LWIP_DHCP_CHECK_LINK_UP (DHCP with Link Up Check)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, dhcp_start() only really starts if the netif has NETIF_FLAG_LINK_UP set in its flags. As this is only an optimization and netif drivers might not set this flag, the default is off. If enabled, netif_set_link_up() must be called to continue dhcp starting. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DHCP_BOOTP_FILE|LWIP_DHCP_BOOTP_FILE (DHCP using Boot File)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, offered_si_addr and boot_file_name are stored. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DHCP_GET_NTP_SRV|LWIP_DHCP_GET_NTP_SRV (DHCP with NTP Server Request)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, request NTP servers with discover/select is performed. For each response packet, an callback is called, which has to be provided by the port: void dhcp_set_ntp_servers(u8_t num_ntp_servers, ip_addr_t* ntp_server_addrs). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DHCP_MAX_NTP_SERVERS|LWIP_DHCP_MAX_NTP_SERVERS (Maximum Number of NTP Servers Requested)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the maximum of NTP servers requested. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DHCP_MAX_DNS_SERVERS|LWIP_DHCP_MAX_DNS_SERVERS (Maximum Number of DNS Servers Requested)|IPv4 - DHCP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the maximum of DNS servers requested. 0: Request DNS servers with discover/select. DHCP servers received in the response are passed to DNS via @ref dns_setserver() (up to the maximum limit defined here). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_AUTOIP|LWIP_AUTOIP (AUTOIP Module)|IPv4 - AUTOIP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, AUTOIP (AUTOmatic linklocal IP) module is enabled.
LWIP_DHCP_AUTOIP_COOP|LWIP_DHCP_AUTOIP_COOP (DHCP/AUTOIP Cooperation Mode)|IPv4 - AUTOIP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, it allows DHCP and AUTOIP to be both enabled on the same interface at the same time.
LWIP_DHCP_AUTOIP_COOP_TRIES|LWIP_DHCP_AUTOIP_COOP_TRIES (DHCP AUTOIP Coop Tries)|IPv4 - AUTOIP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter sets to the number of DHCP DISCOVER probes that should be sent before falling back on AUTOIP (the DHCP client keeps running in this case). This can be set as low as 1 to get an AutoIP address very quickly, but you should be prepared to handle a changing IP address when DHCP overrides AutoIP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_MIB2_CALLBACKS|LWIP_MIB2_CALLBACKS (MIB2 Callbacks Support)|Infrastructure - SNMP MIB2 Callbacks Options||||When this parameter is enabled, this turn on SNMP MIB2 callbacks. Turn this on to get callbacks needed to implement MIB2. Usually MIB2_STATS should be enabled, too. Please read the snmp_agent.txt before turning on. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DNS_TABLE_SIZE|DNS_TABLE_SIZE (DNS Maximum Number of Entries)|Callback - DNS Options||0|2147483647|DNS maximum number of entries to maintain locally. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DNS_MAX_NAME_LENGTH|DNS_MAX_NAME_LENGTH (DNS Maximum Host Name Length)|Callback - DNS Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|DNS maximum host name length supported in the name table. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DNS_MAX_SERVERS|DNS_MAX_SERVERS (Maximum Number of DNS Servers)|Callback - DNS Options||0|2147483647|The maximum of DNS servers. The first server can be initialized automatically by defining DNS_SERVER_ADDRESS(ipaddr), where ipaddr is an ip_addr_t*. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DNS_DOES_NAME_CHECK|DNS_DOES_NAME_CHECK (DNS Name Checking)|Callback - DNS Options||||DNS do a name checking between the query and the response. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DNS_SECURE|LWIP_DNS_SECURE (DNS Security Level Control)|Callback - DNS Options||0|2147483647|This parameter controls the security level of the DNS implementation. All DNS security features are used by default performed thanks to a logical OR: LWIP_DNS_SECURE_RAND_XID / LWIP_DNS_SECURE_NO_MULTIPLE_OUTSTANDING / LWIP_DNS_SECURE_RAND_SRC_PORT. This is overridable but should only be needed by very small targets or when using against non standard DNS servers. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DNS_SECURE_RAND_XID|LWIP_DNS_SECURE_RAND_XID (DNS Security Random)|Callback - DNS Options||||This parameter sets the DNS random security level. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DNS_SECURE_NO_MULTIPLE_OUTSTANDING|LWIP_DNS_SECURE_NO_MULTIPLE_OUTSTANDING (DNS Security without Multiple Outstanding)|Callback - DNS Options||0|2147483647|This parameter sets the DNS security level without multiple outstanding. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DNS_SECURE_RAND_SRC_PORT|LWIP_DNS_SECURE_RAND_SRC_PORT (DNS Security Random Source Port)|Callback - DNS Options||0|2147483647|This parameter sets the DNS security level with random source port. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST|DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST (DNS Local host-to-address List Implementation)|Callback - DNS Options||||When this parameter is enabled, a local host-to-address list is implemented. If enabled, you have to define an initializer DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_INIT to {DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_ELEM("host_ip4", IPADDR4_INIT_BYTES(1,2,3,4)),DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_ELEM("host_ip6", IPADDR6_INIT_HOST(123, 234, 345, 456)}. This additional definition can be added inside a user section within lwipopts.h file Instead, you can also use an external function: define DNS_LOOKUP_LOCAL_EXTERN(x) extern err_t my_lookup_function(const char *name, ip_addr_t *addr, u8_t dns_addrtype) that looks up the IP address and returns ERR_OK if found (LWIP_DNS_ADDRTYPE_xxx is passed in dns_addrtype).
DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_IS_DYNAMIC|DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_IS_DYNAMIC (Dynamic DNS Local host-to-address List)|Callback - DNS Options||||If this is turned on, the local host-list can be dynamically changed at runtime. <b>Dependency</b>: Use DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST requires DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_INIT or DNS_LOCAL_HOSTLIST_IS_DYNAMIC defined.
LWIP_DNS_SUPPORT_MDNS_QUERIES|LWIP_DNS_SUPPORT_MDNS_QUERIES (Use Multicast DNS Query)|Callback - DNS Options||||Set this to 1 to enable querying ".local" names via mDNS using a One-Shot Multicast DNS Query. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_UDPLITE|LWIP_UDPLITE (UDP-Lite Module)|Callback - UDP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, it turns on UDP-Lite (Requires LWIP_UDP).
UDP_TTL|UDP_TTL (Number of Time-To-Live Used by UDP Packets)|Callback - UDP Options|Node(s)|0|255|Default UDP Time-To-Live value. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETBUF_RECVINFO|LWIP_NETBUF_RECVINFO (Append Destination and Port Addresses to every Netbuf)|Callback - UDP Options||||Append destination addr and port to every netbuf. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_TTL|TCP_TTL (Number of Time-To-Live Used by TCP Packets)|Callback - TCP Options|Node(s)|0|255|Default TCP Time-To-Live value. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_WND|TCP_WND (TCP Receive Window Maximum Size)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|=IF(!LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_TCP, TCP_MSS, 0)|=MIN(MIN(IF(LWIP_WND_SCALE & LWIP_TCP,4294967295,4294967295),IF(LWIP_TCP,65535,4294967295)),MIN(IF(LWIP_WND_SCALE & LWIP_TCP,65535*INTPOW(2,TCP_RCV_SCALE),4294967295),IF(LWIP_TCP & !MEMP_MEM_MALLOC & PBUF_POOL_SIZE & !LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS,PBUF_POOL_SIZE*(PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE-(PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN+PBUF_LINK_HLEN+PBUF_IP_HLEN+PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN)),4294967295)))|Size of a TCP window. <b>Warning</b>: when using TCP_RCV_SCALE, TCP_WND is the total size with scaling applied. Maximum window value in the TCP header will be TCP_WND >> TCP_RCV_SCALE. <b>Dependency</b>: TCP_WND Min and Max values have some dependencies with other parameters (see init.c file for all details): If you use TCP_WND with LWIP_TCP enabled: - The minimum value advised by the stack is 2*TCP_MSS - TCP_WND must fit in an u16_t or you have to enable window scaling If you use TCP_WND with LWIP_TCP and LWIP_WND_SCALE enabled: - TCP_WND must fit in an u32_t - TCP_WND must be lower or equals than (0xFFFFU << TCP_RCV_SCALE) If you use TCP_WND with LWIP_TCP enabled and MEMP_MEM_MALLOC and LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS are disabled:- TCP_WND must be lower or equal than PBUF_POOL_SIZE*(PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE-(PBUF_LINK_ENCAPSULATION_HLEN + PBUF_LINK_HLEN + PBUF_IP_HLEN + PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN)) If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1.
TCP_MAXRTX|TCP_MAXRTX (Maximum Number of Retransmissions of Data Segments)|Callback - TCP Options||0|=IF(!LWIP_TCP, 1000000, 12)|Maximum number of retransmissions of data segments. <b>Dependency</b>: If LWIP_TCP=1, TCP_MAXRTX must less or equal to 12.
TCP_SYNMAXRTX|TCP_SYNMAXRTX (Maximum Number of Retransmissions of SYN Segments)|Callback - TCP Options||0|=IF(!LWIP_TCP, 1000000, 12)|Maximum number of retransmissions of SYN segments. <b>Dependency</b>: If LWIP_TCP=1, TCP_SYNMAXRTX must less or equal to 12.
TCP_QUEUE_OOSEQ|TCP_QUEUE_OOSEQ (Allow Out-Of-Order Incoming Packets)|Callback - TCP Options||||If this parameter is enabled, TCP will queue segments that arrive out of order. Define to 0 if your device is low on memory. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_MSS|TCP_MSS (Maximum Segment Size)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|1||TCP Maximum segment size. Default value is 536, this is a conservative value, you might want to increase this. For the receive side, this MSS is advertised to the remote side when opening a connection. For the transmit size, this MSS sets an upper limit on the MSS advertised by the remote host. <b>Warning</b>: With Ethernet, the maximum size of a TCP segment allowed is 1460. To handle corner cases, the maximum value is also limited to: PBUF_POOL_SIZE / 2 * (PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE - (PBUF_LINK_HLEN + PBUF_IP_HLEN + PBUF_TRANSPORT_HLEN)).
TCP_CALCULATE_EFF_SEND_MSS|TCP_CALCULATE_EFF_SEND_MSS (Checks TCP_MSS against NETIF MTU Used)|Callback - TCP Options||||Maximum size of a segment that TCP really sends, the 'effective send MSS,' MUST be the smaller of the send MSS (which reflects the available reassembly buffer size at the remote host) and the largest size permitted by the IP layer (RFC 1122) Setting this to 1 enables code that checks TCP_MSS against the MTU of the netif used for a connection and limits the MSS if it would be too big otherwise. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_SND_BUF|TCP_SND_BUF (TCP Sender Buffer Space)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|=IF(!LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_TCP, TCP_MSS*2, 0)|2147483647|TCP sender buffer space (bytes). To achieve good performance. <b>Dependency</b>: Use TCP_SND_BUF requires TCP enabled. When LWIP_TCP is enabled, TCP_SND_BUF must be at least as much as (2 * TCP_MSS) for things to work smoothly. If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1 to avoid Min value limitation. TCP_SND_BUF value is also used to set value of TCP_SND_QUEUELEN and TCP_SNDLOWAT.
TCP_SND_QUEUELEN|TCP_SND_QUEUELEN (Number of Packet Buffers Allowed for TCP Sender)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|=MAX(IF(LWIP_TCP, 2, 0), IF(!LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_TCP,2*TCP_SND_BUF/TCP_MSS,0))|=IF(LWIP_TCP, 65535, 2147483647)|TCP sender buffer space (pbufs). <b>Dependency</b>: TCP_SND_QUEUELEN must be at least as much as (2*TCP_SND_BUF/TCP_MSS) if LWIP_TCP is enabled and LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS disabled. TCP_SND_QUEUELEN must be at least 2 for no-copy TCP writes to work, if LWIP_TCP is enabled. TCP_SND_QUEUELEN must fit in an u16_t, if LWIP_TCP is enabled.
TCP_SNDLOWAT|TCP_SNDLOWAT (TCP Writable Space)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|0|=IF(!LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_TCP, MIN(TCP_SND_BUF-1,65535-(4*TCP_MSS)-1), 2147483647)|TCP writable space (bytes). This must be less than TCP_SND_BUF. It is the amount of space which must be available in the TCP snd_buf for select to return writable (combined with TCP_SNDQUEUELOWAT). <b>Dependency</b>: TCP_SNDLOWAT is forced to be less than TCP_SND_BUF when LWIP_TCP is enabled and LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS is disabled. If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1 to disable the Max value limitation.
TCP_SNDQUEUELOWAT|TCP_SNDQUEUELOWAT (TCP Writable Buffers)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|0|=IF(!LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS & LWIP_TCP, TCP_SND_QUEUELEN-1, 1000000)|TCP writable bufs (pbuf count). This must be less than TCP_SND_QUEUELEN. If the number of pbufs queued on a pcb drops below this number, select returns writable (combined with TCP_SNDLOWAT). <b>Dependency</b>: TCP_SNDQUEUELOWAT must be less than TCP_SND_QUEUELEN when LWIP_TCP is enabled and LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS is disabled. If you know what you are doing, define LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS to 1 to disable the Max value limitation.
TCP_OOSEQ_MAX_BYTES|TCP_OOSEQ_MAX_BYTES (Maximum Number of Bytes Queued on ooseq per Pcb)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Maximum number of bytes queued on ooseq per pcb. Default is 0 (no limit). Only valid for TCP_QUEUE_OOSEQ=1. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_OOSEQ_MAX_PBUFS|TCP_OOSEQ_MAX_PBUFS (Maximum Number of Pbufs Queued on ooseq per Pcb)|Callback - TCP Options||0|2147483647|Maximum number of pbufs queued on ooseq per pcb. Default is 0 (no limit). Only valid for TCP_QUEUE_OOSEQ=1. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG|TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG (Backlog for TCP Listen Pcb)|Callback - TCP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, the backlog option for tcp listen pcb is set.
TCP_DEFAULT_LISTEN_BACKLOG|TCP_DEFAULT_LISTEN_BACKLOG (Maximum Allowed Backlog for TCP Listen Netconns)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|0|=IF(LWIP_TCP & TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG, 255, 1000000)|Maximum allowed backlog for TCP listen netconns. This backlog is used unless another is explicitly specified. 0xff is the maximum (u8_t). <b>Dependency</b>: Link with LWIP_TCP and TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG parameters: when LWIP_TCP and TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG are enabled, TCP_DEFAULT_LISTEN_BACKLOG must less than 256.
TCP_OVERSIZE|TCP_OVERSIZE (Maximum Number of Bytes that tcp_write)|Callback - TCP Options||||Maximum number of bytes that tcp_write may allocate ahead of time in an attempt to create shorter pbuf chains for transmission. The meaningful range is 0 to TCP_MSS. Some suggested values are: - 0: Disable oversized allocation. Each tcp_write() allocates a new pbuf (old behaviour) - 1: Allocate size-aligned pbufs with minimal excess. Use this if your scatter-gather DMA requires aligned fragments - 128: Limit the pbuf/memory overhead to 20% - TCP_MSS: Try to create unfragmented TCP packets - TCP_MSS/4: Try to create 4 fragments or less per TCP packet. <b>Dependency</b>: If TCP_OVERSIZE>0 LWIP_NETIF_TX_SINGLE_PBUF can be used.
LWIP_TCP_TIMESTAMPS|LWIP_TCP_TIMESTAMPS (TCP Iimestamp)|Callback - TCP Options||||Support the TCP timestamp option. The timestamp option is currently only used to help remote hosts, it is not really used locally. Therefore, it is only enabled when a TS option is received in the initial SYN packet from a remote host. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_WND_UPDATE_THRESHOLD|TCP_WND_UPDATE_THRESHOLD (TCP Window Update Threshold)|Callback - TCP Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Difference in window to trigger an explicit window update. <b>Dependency</b>: TCP_WND_UPDATE_THRESHOLD is the min between (TCP_WND / 4) and (TCP_MSS * 4).
LWIP_EVENT_API|LWIP_EVENT_API (LwIP Event API)|Callback - TCP Options||||LWIP_EVENT_API=1: The user defines lwip_tcp_event() to receive all events (accept, sent, etc) that happen in the system.
LWIP_CALLBACK_API|LWIP_CALLBACK_API (LwIP CallBack API)|Callback - TCP Options||||LWIP_CALLBACK_API=1: The PCB callback function is called directly for the event. This is the default.
LWIP_WND_SCALE|LWIP_WND_SCALE (Window Scaling Support)|Callback - TCP Options||||Set LWIP_WND_SCALE to 1 to enable window scaling. When LWIP_WND_SCALE is enabled but TCP_RCV_SCALE is 0, we can use a large send window while having a small receive window only. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_RCV_SCALE|TCP_RCV_SCALE (Receive Scaling Factor Support)|Callback - TCP Options||0|=IF(LWIP_TCP,14,2147483647)|Set TCP_RCV_SCALE to the desired scaling factor, shift count in the range of 0..14. When LWIP_WND_SCALE is enabled but TCP_RCV_SCALE is 0, we can use a large send window while having a small receive window only. <b>Dependency</b>: If LWIP_TCP is enabled, maximum valid window scale value is 14.
LWIP_NETIF_HOSTNAME|LWIP_NETIF_HOSTNAME (NETIF Hostname)|Network Interfaces Options||||This option uses DHCP_OPTION_HOSTNAME with netif's hostname field. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_HOSTNAME_NAME|LWIP_NETIF_HOSTNAME_NAME (LWIP_NETIF_HOSTNAME_NAME (LWIP NETIF Hostname))|Network Interfaces Options||||User can specify the LwIP NETIF Hostname. Default name is "lwip".
LWIP_NETIF_API|LWIP_NETIF_API (Network Interfaces API)|Network Interfaces Options||||When this parameter is set, netif api is supported (in netifapi.c).
LWIP_NETIF_STATUS_CALLBACK|LWIP_NETIF_STATUS_CALLBACK (Callback Function on Interface Status Changes)|Network Interfaces Options||||Set parameter allow to support a callback function whenever an interface changes its up/down status (i.e., due to DHCP IP acquisition). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_LINK_CALLBACK|LWIP_NETIF_LINK_CALLBACK (Callback Function on Interface Link Changes)|Network Interfaces Options||||Set parameter allow to support a callback function from an interface whenever the link changes (i.e., link down). Since CubeMx 6.0.0 release, It is enabled like in LwIP Cube FW applications. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_REMOVE_CALLBACK|LWIP_NETIF_REMOVE_CALLBACK (Callback Function on Interface Removal)|Network Interfaces Options||||Set parameter allow to support a callback function that is called when a netif has been removed. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_HWADDRHINT|LWIP_NETIF_HWADDRHINT (Cache Link-layer-address Hints)|Network Interfaces Options||||Set parameter allow to support cache link-layer-address hints (e.g. table indices) in struct netif. TCP and UDP can make use of this to prevent scanning the ARP table for every sent packet. While this is faster for big ARP tables or many concurrent connections, it might be counterproductive if you have a tiny ARP table or if there never are concurrent connections. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_TX_SINGLE_PBUF|LWIP_NETIF_TX_SINGLE_PBUF (Put Data into One Single Pbuf)|Network Interfaces Options||||When this parameter is enabled, lwIP tries to put all data to be sent into one single pbuf. This is for compatibility with DMA-enabled MACs that do not support scatter-gather. Beware that this might involve CPU-memcpy before transmitting that would not be needed without this flag! Use this only if you need to! <b>Warning</b>: TCP and IP-frag do not work with this yet.
LWIP_NUM_NETIF_CLIENT_DATA|LWIP_NUM_NETIF_CLIENT_DATA (Cache Link-layer-address Hints)|Network Interfaces Options||0|2147483647|Number of clients that may store data in client_data member array of struct netif. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_HAVE_LOOPIF|LWIP_HAVE_LOOPIF (Loop Interface)|NETIF - Loopback Interface Options||||When this parameter is enabled, LwIP supports loop interface (127.0.0.1). This is only needed when no real netifs are available. If at least one other netif is available, loopback traffic uses this netif. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_LOOPIF_MULTICAST|LWIP_LOOPIF_MULTICAST (Multicast/IGMP on Loop Interface)|NETIF - Loopback Interface Options||||When this parameter is enabled, LwIP supports multicast/IGMP on loop interface (127.0.0.1). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK|LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK (NETIF Loopback)|NETIF - Loopback Interface Options||||Support sending packets with a destination IP address equal to the netif IP address, looping them back up the stack. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_LOOPBACK_MAX_PBUFS|LWIP_LOOPBACK_MAX_PBUFS (Maximum Number of Pbufs for Loopback Sending)|NETIF - Loopback Interface Options||0|2147483647|Maximum number of pbufs on queue for loopback sending for each netif (0 = disabled). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK_MULTITHREADING|LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK_MULTITHREADING (Multi-threading)|NETIF - Loopback Interface Options||||This option indicates whether threading is enabled in the system, as netifs must change how they behave depending on this setting for the LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK option to work. Setting this is needed to avoid reentering non-reentrant functions like tcp_input(). LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK_MULTITHREADING=1: Indicates that the user is using a multithreaded environment like tcpip.c. In this case, netif->input() is called directly. LWIP_NETIF_LOOPBACK_MULTITHREADING=0: Indicates a polling (or NO_SYS=1) setup. The packets are put on a list and netif_poll() must be called in the main application loop.
TCPIP_THREAD_NAME|TCPIP_THREAD_NAME (TCPIP Thread Name)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||||Name assigned to the main tcpip thread.
TCPIP_THREAD_STACKSIZE|TCPIP_THREAD_STACKSIZE (TCPIP Thread Stack Size)|Infrastructure - Threading Options|Word(s)|=FREERTOS$context:configMINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|=FREERTOS$context:MAX_SIZE_IN_USE|Stack size used by the main tcpip thread (Word 32 bits). The stack size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_thread_new() when the thread is created.
TCPIP_THREAD_PRIO|TCPIP_THREAD_PRIO (TCPIP Thread Priority Level)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|=FREERTOS$context:configMAX_PRIORITIES-1|Priority assigned to the main tcpip thread. The priority value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_thread_new() when the thread is created.
TCPIP_MBOX_SIZE|TCPIP_MBOX_SIZE (TCPIP Mailbox Size)|Infrastructure - Threading Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Mailbox size for the tcpip thread messages. The queue size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_mbox_new() when tcpip_init is called. To be functional, the default value (0 in opt.h) is defined to 6 like in CubeMX Firmware packages LwIP examples.
SLIPIF_THREAD_NAME|SLIPIF_THREAD_NAME (TCPIP SLIPIF Loop Thread Name)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||||Name assigned to the slipif_loop thread.
SLIPIF_THREAD_STACKSIZE|SLIPIF_THREAD_STACKSIZE (SLIPIF Thread Stack Size)|Infrastructure - Threading Options|Word(s)|=FREERTOS$context:configMINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|=FREERTOS$context:MAX_SIZE_IN_USE|Stack size used by the slipif_loop thread. The stack size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_thread_new() when the thread is created.
SLIPIF_THREAD_PRIO|SLIPIF_THREAD_PRIO (SLIPIF Thread Priority Level)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|=FREERTOS$context:configMAX_PRIORITIES-1|Priority assigned to the slipif_loop thread. The priority value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_thread_new() when the thread is created.
DEFAULT_THREAD_NAME|DEFAULT_THREAD_NAME (Default LwIP Thread Name)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||||Name assigned to any other lwIP thread.
DEFAULT_THREAD_STACKSIZE|DEFAULT_THREAD_STACKSIZE (Default LwIP Thread Stack Size)|Infrastructure - Threading Options|Word(s)|=FREERTOS$context:configMINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|=FREERTOS$context:MAX_SIZE_IN_USE|Stack size used by any other lwIP thread. The stack size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_thread_new() when the thread is created.
DEFAULT_THREAD_PRIO|DEFAULT_THREAD_PRIO (Default LwIP Thread Priority Level)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|=FREERTOS$context:configMAX_PRIORITIES-1|Priority assigned to any other lwIP thread. The priority value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_thread_new() when the thread is created.
DEFAULT_RAW_RECVMBOX_SIZE|DEFAULT_RAW_RECVMBOX_SIZE (Default Mailbox Size on a NETCONN Raw)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|2147483647|Mailbox size for the incoming packets on a NETCONN_RAW. The queue size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_mbox_new() when the recvmbox is created.
DEFAULT_UDP_RECVMBOX_SIZE|DEFAULT_UDP_RECVMBOX_SIZE (Default Mailbox Size on a NETCONN UDP)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|2147483647|Mailbox size for the incoming packets on a NETCONN_UDP. The queue size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_mbox_new() when the recvmbox is created. To be functional, the default value (0 in opt.h) is defined to 6 like in CubeMX Firmware packages LwIP examples, if LWIP_UDP is enabled.
DEFAULT_TCP_RECVMBOX_SIZE|DEFAULT_TCP_RECVMBOX_SIZE (Default Mailbox Size on a NETCONN TCP)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|2147483647|Mailbox size for the incoming packets on a NETCONN_TCP. The queue size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_mbox_new() when the recvmbox is created. To be functional, the default value (0 in opt.h) is defined to 6 like in CubeMX Firmware packages LwIP examples, if LWIP_TCP is enabled.
DEFAULT_ACCEPTMBOX_SIZE|DEFAULT_ACCEPTMBOX_SIZE (Default Mailbox Size for Incoming Connections)|Infrastructure - Threading Options||0|2147483647|Mailbox size for the incoming connections. The queue size value itself is platform-dependent, but is passed to sys_mbox_new() when the acceptmbox is created. To be functional, the default value (0 in opt.h) is defined to 6 like in CubeMX Firmware packages LwIP examples.
LWIP_NETCONN|LWIP_NETCONN (NETCONN API)|Thread Safe APIs - Netconn Options||||When this parameter is enabled, Netconn API is enabled (require to use api_lib.c).
LWIP_TCPIP_TIMEOUT|LWIP_TCPIP_TIMEOUT (Use TCPIP Timeout)|Thread Safe APIs - Netconn Options||||Enable tcpip_timeout/tcpip_untimeout to create timers running in tcpip_thread from another thread. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETCONN_SEM_PER_THREAD|LWIP_NETCONN_SEM_PER_THREAD (Netconn uses One Semaphore per Thread)|Thread Safe APIs - Netconn Options||||Enable this parameter to use one (thread-local) semaphore per thread calling socket/netconn functions instead of allocating one semaphore per netconn (and per select etc.) ATTENTION: a thread-local semaphore for API calls is needed: - LWIP_NETCONN_THREAD_SEM_GET() returning a sys_sem_t - LWIP_NETCONN_THREAD_SEM_ALLOC() creating the semaphore - LWIP_NETCONN_THREAD_SEM_FREE() freeing the semaphore The latter 2 can be invoked up by calling netconn_thread_init()/netconn_thread_cleanup(). Ports may call these for threads created with sys_thread_new(). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_NETCONN_FULLDUPLEX|LWIP_NETCONN_FULLDUPLEX (Netconn in Full Duplex)|Thread Safe APIs - Netconn Options||||This parameter enables code that allows reading from one thread, writing from a 2nd thread and closing from a 3rd thread at the same time. ATTENTION: This is currently really alpha! Some requirements: - LWIP_NETCONN_SEM_PER_THREAD==1 is required to use one socket/netconn from multiple threads at once - sys_mbox_free() has to unblock receive tasks waiting on recvmbox/acceptmbox and prevent a task pending on this during/after deletion. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_SOCKET|LWIP_SOCKET (Socket API)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||When this parameter is enabled, Socket API is enabled (require to use sockets.c)
LWIP_COMPAT_SOCKETS|LWIP_COMPAT_SOCKETS (BSD-style Socket Functions Names)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||0|2|LWIP_COMPAT_SOCKETS==1: Enable BSD-style sockets functions names through defines. LWIP_COMPAT_SOCKETS==2: Same as ==1 but correctly named functions are created. While this helps code completion, it might conflict with existing libraries (only used if you use sockets.c).
LWIP_SOCKET_SET_ERRNO|LWIP_SOCKET_SET_ERRNO (Socket Error Number)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||This parameter allows to set an error number (errno) when socket functions cannot complete successfully, as required by POSIX. Default is POSIX-compliant.
LWIP_POSIX_SOCKETS_IO_NAMES|LWIP_POSIX_SOCKETS_IO_NAMES (POSIX-style Sockets Functions Names)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Enable POSIX-style sockets functions names. Disable this option if you use a POSIX operating system that uses the same names (read, write and close). (only used if you use sockets.c).
LWIP_SOCKET_OFFSET|LWIP_SOCKET_OFFSET (Socket Offset Number)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||0|2147483647|LWIP_SOCKET_OFFSET==n: Increases the file descriptor number created by LwIP with n. This can be useful when there are multiple APIs which create file descriptors. When they all start with a different offset and you won't make them overlap you can re implement read/write/close/ioctl/fnctl to send the requested action to the right library (sharing select will need more work though).
LWIP_TCP_KEEPALIVE|LWIP_TCP_KEEPALIVE (TCP_KEEPIDLE, TCP_KEEPINTVL and TCP_KEEPCNT Options)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Enable TCP_KEEPIDLE, TCP_KEEPINTVL and TCP_KEEPCNT options processing. Note that TCP_KEEPIDLE and TCP_KEEPINTVL have to be set in seconds (does not require sockets.c, and will affect tcp.c).
LWIP_SO_SNDTIMEO|LWIP_SO_SNDTIMEO (Send Timeout for Socket/Netconns)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Enable send timeout for sockets/netconns and SO_SNDTIMEO processing.
LWIP_SO_RCVTIMEO|LWIP_SO_RCVTIMEO (Receive Timeout for Socket/Netconns)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Enable receive timeout for sockets/netconns and SO_RCVTIMEO processing.
LWIP_SO_SNDRCVTIMEO_NONSTANDARD|LWIP_SO_SNDRCVTIMEO_NONSTANDARD (Send/Receive Non Standard Timeout)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||When this parameter is enabled, SO_RCVTIMEO/SO_SNDTIMEO take an int (milliseconds, much like winsock does) instead of a struct timeval (default).
LWIP_SO_RCVBUF|LWIP_SO_RCVBUF (SO_RCVBUF Processing)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Enable SO_RCVBUF processing.
LWIP_SO_LINGER|LWIP_SO_LINGER (SO_LINGER Processing)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||This paarmeter enables SO_LINGER processing.
RECV_BUFSIZE_DEFAULT|RECV_BUFSIZE_DEFAULT (SO_RCVBUF Size Value)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||0|2147483647|If LWIP_SO_RCVBUF is used, this is the default value for recv_bufsize.
LWIP_TCP_CLOSE_TIMEOUT_MS_DEFAULT|LWIP_TCP_CLOSE_TIMEOUT_MS_DEFAULT (SO_LINGER Processing)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||0|2147483647|If set by default, TCP socket/netconn close waits 20 seconds max to send the FIN.
SO_REUSE|SO_REUSE (SO_REUSEADDR Option)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Enable SO_REUSEADDR option.
SO_REUSE_RXTOALL|SO_REUSE_RXTOALL (Pass Copy of Incoming Broadcast/Multicast Packets)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||Pass a copy of incoming broadcast/multicast packets to all local matches if SO_REUSEADDR is turned on. <b>Warning</b>: Adds a memcpy for every packet if passing to more than one pcb!
LWIP_FIONREAD_LINUXMODE|LWIP_FIONREAD_LINUXMODE (Window verus Linux Pending Data)|Thread Safe APIs - Socket Options||||By default, LWIP_FIONREAD_LINUXMODE is disabled: ioctl/FIONREAD returns the amount of pending data in the network buffer. This is the way windows does it. It's the default for lwIP since it is smaller. When LWIP_FIONREAD_LINUXMODE is enabled: ioctl/FIONREAD returns the size of the next pending datagram in bytes. This is the way linux does it. This code is only here for compatibility.
LWIP_USE_EXTERNAL_POLARSSL|LWIP_USE_EXTERNAL_POLARSSL (Use external PolarSSL library)|PolarSSL Options||||Enable this option to use external PolarSSL library. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_USE_EXTERNAL_MBEDTLS|LWIP_USE_EXTERNAL_MBEDTLS (Use external mbed TLS library)|PolarSSL Options||||Enable this option to use mbed TLS library. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_MD5|LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_MD5 (LwIP internal PolarSSL for MD5)|PolarSSL Options||||This parameter enabled allows to use lwIP internal PolarSSL for MD5.
LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_MD4|LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_MD4 (LwIP internal PolarSSL for MD4)|PolarSSL Options||||This parameter enabled allows to use lwIP internal PolarSSL for MD4.
LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_SHA1|LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_SHA1 (LwIP internal PolarSSL for SHA1)|PolarSSL Options||||This parameter enabled allows to use lwIP internal PolarSSL for SHA1.
LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_DES|LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_DES (LwIP internal PolarSSL for DES)|PolarSSL Options||||This parameter enabled allows to use lwIP internal PolarSSL for DES.
LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_ARC4|LWIP_INCLUDED_POLARSSL_ARC4 (LwIP internal PolarSSL for ARC4)|PolarSSL Options||||This parameter enabled allows to use lwIP internal PolarSSL for ARC4.
PPP_SUPPORT|PPP_SUPPORT (PPP Module)|PPP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) is enabled.
PPPOE_SUPPORT|PPPOE_SUPPORT (PPP Over Ethernet)|PPP Options||||Enable PPP Over Ethernet. <b>Warning</b>: Enable PPP requires user specific code not generated by STM32Cube MX. This is reserved for advanced users.
PPPOL2TP_SUPPORT|PPPOL2TP_SUPPORT (PPP Over L2TP)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP Over L2TP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPPOL2TP_AUTH_SUPPORT|PPPOL2TP_AUTH_SUPPORT (PPP Over L2TP Auth)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP Over L2TP Auth (enable MD5 support). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPPOS_SUPPORT|PPPOS_SUPPORT (PPP Over Serial)|PPP Options||||Enable PPP Over Serial. <b>Dependency</b>: PPP_SUPPORT needs either PPPOS_SUPPORT or PPPOE_SUPPORT turned on. <b>Warning</b>: Enable PPP requires user specific code not generated by STM32Cube MX. This is reserved for advanced users.
LWIP_PPP_API|LWIP_PPP_API (PPP API)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP API (in pppapi.c). It is by default enabled if NO_SYS equals 0 and if PPP_SUPPORT is enabled.
MEMP_NUM_PPP_PCB|MEMP_NUM_PPP_PCB (Number of Simultaneously Active PPP Connections)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Number of simultaneously active PPP connections (requires the PPP_SUPPORT option)
MEMP_NUM_PPPOS_INTERFACES|MEMP_NUM_PPPOS_INTERFACES (Number of Concurrent Active PPPoS Interfaces)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active PPPoS interfaces (only used with PPPOS_SUPPORT enabled)
MEMP_NUM_PPPOE_INTERFACES|MEMP_NUM_PPPOE_INTERFACES (Number of Concurrent Active PPPoE Interfaces)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active PPPoE interfaces (only used with PPPOE_SUPPORT enabled)
MEMP_NUM_PPPOL2TP_INTERFACES|MEMP_NUM_PPPOL2TP_INTERFACES (Number of Concurrent Active PPPoL2TP Interfaces)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Number of concurrently active PPPoL2TP interfaces (only used with PPPOL2TP_SUPPORT enabled)
MEMP_NUM_PPP_API_MSG|MEMP_NUM_PPP_API_MSG (Number of Concurrent PPP API messages)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Number of concurrent PPP API messages (in pppapi.c)
PPP_INPROC_IRQ_SAFE|PPP_INPROC_IRQ_SAFE (PPP Input Safe Interrupt)|PPP Options||||When this parameter is enabled, pppos_input() is called using tcpip_callback(). Please read the "PPPoS input path" chapter in the PPP documentation about this option. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PRINTPKT_SUPPORT|PRINTPKT_SUPPORT (PPP Print Packet)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP print packet support. Mandatory for debugging, it displays exchanged packet content in debug trace. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_IPV4_SUPPORT|PPP_IPV4_SUPPORT (PPP IPv4)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP IPv4 support. By default, this parameter is set with LWIP_IPV4.
PPP_IPV6_SUPPORT|PPP_IPV6_SUPPORT (PPP IPv6)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP IPv6 support. By default, this parameter is set with LWIP_IPV6. <b>Dependency</b>: PPP_IPV6_SUPPORT needs LWIP_IPV6 turned on.
PPP_NOTIFY_PHASE|PPP_NOTIFY_PHASE (PPP Notify Phase)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP notify phase support. PPP notify phase support allows you to set a callback which is called on change of the internal PPP state machine. This can be used for example to set a LED pattern depending on the current phase of the PPP session. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_USE_PBUF_RAM|PPP_USE_PBUF_RAM (PPP use Memory from Heap)|PPP Options||||pbuf_type PPP is using for LCP, PAP, CHAP, EAP, CCP, IPCP and IP6CP packets. Memory allocated must be single buffered for PPP to works, it requires pbuf that are not going to be chained when allocated. This requires setting PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE to at least 512 bytes, which is quite huge for small systems. Setting PPP_USE_PBUF_RAM to 1 makes PPP use memory from heap where continuous buffers are required, allowing you to use a smaller PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_FCS_TABLE|PPP_FCS_TABLE (PPP FCS Table)|PPP Options||||Keep a 256*2 byte table to speed up FCS calculation for PPPoS. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PAP_SUPPORT|PAP_SUPPORT (PAP Module)|PPP Options||||Support Password Authentication Protocol (PAP). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
CHAP_SUPPORT|CHAP_SUPPORT (CHAP Module)|PPP Options||||Support Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol (CHAP). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MSCHAP_SUPPORT|MSCHAP_SUPPORT (MSCHAP Module)|PPP Options||||Support Microsoft version of Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol (MSCHAP). <b>Dependency</b>: Set MSCHAP_SUPPORT requires CHAP_SUPPORT enabled.
EAP_SUPPORT|EAP_SUPPORT (PPP EAP Module)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP EAP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
CCP_SUPPORT|CCP_SUPPORT (PPP CCP Module)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP CCP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MPPE_SUPPORT|MPPE_SUPPORT (MPPE)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables MPPE. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
CBCP_SUPPORT|CBCP_SUPPORT (CBCP Module)|PPP Options||||Support Callback Control Protocol (CBCP). <b>Warning</b>: CURRENTLY NOT SUPPORTED! DO NOT SET! <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ECP_SUPPORT|ECP_SUPPORT (ECP Module)|PPP Options||||Support ECP Control Protocol. <b>Warning</b>: CURRENTLY NOT SUPPORTED! DO NOT SET! <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DEMAND_SUPPORT|DEMAND_SUPPORT (Dial on Demand)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables dial on demand support. <b>Warning</b>: CURRENTLY NOT SUPPORTED! DO NOT SET! <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LQR_SUPPORT|LQR_SUPPORT (PPP Link Quality Report)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP link quality report. Do nothing except exchanging some LCP packets. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_SERVER|PPP_SERVER (PPP Server)|PPP Options||||This parameter enables PPP server support (waiting for incoming PPP session). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_OUR_NAME|PPP_OUR_NAME (PPP Authentication Name)|PPP Options||||This parameter purpose is to set our name for authentication.
VJ_SUPPORT|VJ_SUPPORT (VJ Header Compression)|PPP Options||||Support Van Jacobson (VJ) header compression.
PPP_MD5_RANDM|PPP_MD5_RANDM (PPP MD5 Randomness)|PPP Options||||Use MD5 for better randomness. Enabled by default if CHAP, EAP, or L2TP AUTH support is enabled. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
FSM_DEFTIMEOUT|FSM_DEFTIMEOUT (FSM Timeout)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|Timeout time in seconds. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
FSM_DEFMAXTERMREQS|FSM_DEFMAXTERMREQS (Maximum Number of Terminate-Request Transmissions)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Set the maximum Terminate-Request transmissions. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
FSM_DEFMAXCONFREQS|FSM_DEFMAXCONFREQS (Maximum Number of Configure-Request Transmissions)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Set the Maximum Configure-Request transmissions. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
FSM_DEFMAXNAKLOOPS|FSM_DEFMAXNAKLOOPS (Maximum Number of Nak Loops)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Set the Maximum number of nak loops. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
UPAP_DEFTIMEOUT|UPAP_DEFTIMEOUT (Timeout for Retransmitting Request)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|Set the Timeout (seconds) for retransmitting request with User/Password Authentication Protocol. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
UPAP_DEFTRANSMITS|UPAP_DEFTRANSMITS (Max Times to Send Challenge)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the maximum number of times to send challenge. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
UPAP_DEFREQTIME|UPAP_DEFREQTIME (Time to wait for Authentification Request from Peer)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|Time to wait for auth-req from peer with User/Password Authentication Protocol. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
CHAP_DEFTIMEOUT|CHAP_DEFTIMEOUT (CHAP Timeout)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|CHAP (Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol) Timeout time in seconds. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
CHAP_DEFTRANSMITS|CHAP_DEFTRANSMITS (CHAP Maximum Times to send Challenge)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Max times to send challenge with Challenge Handshake Authentication Protocol. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
CHAP_DEFRECHALLENGETIME|CHAP_DEFRECHALLENGETIME (Rechallenge Peer Time)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|If this option is > 0, rechallenge the peer every n seconds.
EAP_DEFREQTIME|EAP_DEFREQTIME (Time to Wait for Peer Request)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|This parameter allows to set the time to wait for peer request. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
EAP_DEFALLOWREQ|EAP_DEFALLOWREQ (Maximum Times to Accept Requests)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter allows to set the times to accept requests. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
EAP_DEFTIMEOUT|EAP_DEFTIMEOUT (Timeout for Rexmit)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|This parameter allows to set a timeout (seconds) for rexmit.
EAP_DEFTRANSMITS|EAP_DEFTRANSMITS (Maximum Times to Transmit)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter allows to set maximum number of times to transmit.
LCP_DEFLOOPBACKFAIL|LCP_DEFLOOPBACKFAIL (Loopback Detection Timing)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Default number of times we receive our magic number from the peer before deciding the link is looped-back. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LCP_ECHOINTERVAL|LCP_ECHOINTERVAL (Interval between Keepalive Echo Requests)|PPP Options|Second(s)|0|2147483647|Interval in seconds between keepalive echo requests, 0 to disable in Link Control Protocol. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LCP_MAXECHOFAILS|LCP_MAXECHOFAILS (Number of Unanswered Echo Requests before Failure)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Number of unanswered echo requests before failure in Link Control Protocol. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_MAXIDLEFLAG|PPP_MAXIDLEFLAG (Maximum Xmit Idle Time before Resend Flag Char)|PPP Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|Max Xmit idle time (in ms) before resend flag char. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_MRU|PPP_MRU (Maximum Length of MRU Info Field)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Default MRU = max length of info field. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_DEFMRU|PPP_DEFMRU (Number of Negotiated MRU)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Maximum negotiated Maximum Receive Unit (MRU) mandated by PPP protocol. <b>Warning</b>: Try for this! <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_MAXMRU|PPP_MAXMRU (Maximum Number of MRU Allowed)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Largest MRU allowed. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_MINMRU|PPP_MINMRU (Minimum Number of MRU Allowed)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|Minimum negotiated Maximum Receive Unit (MRU) mandated by PPP protocol. <b>Warning</b>: No MRUs below this. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPPOL2TP_DEFMRU|PPPOL2TP_DEFMRU (Default MTU and MRU for L2TP)|PPP Options||0|2147483647|This parameter sets MTU and MRU for L2TP. The default value (1450) equals: 1500 - PPPoE(6) - PPP Protocol(2) - IPv4 header(20) - UDP Header(8) - L2TP Header(6) - HDLC Header(2) - PPP Protocol(2) - MPPE Header(2) - PPP Protocol(2).
MAXNAMELEN|MAXNAMELEN (Maximum Length of Hostname or Name for Authentification)|PPP Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Maximum length of hostname or name for auth. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MAXSECRETLEN|MAXSECRETLEN (Maximum Length of Password)|PPP Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|Maximum length of password or secret. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6|LWIP_IPV6 (IPv6 Protocol)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter enables IPv6 protocol. <b>Warning</b>: When using IGMP or IPv6, LWIP_RAND() needs to be defined to a random-function returning an u32_t random value (in arch/cc.h). Therefore, LWIP_RAND() has been added in cc.h (in \system\arch). <b>Note: </b>All other IPv6 options are visible as "Advanced Parameters". <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_NUM_ADDRESSES|LWIP_IPV6_NUM_ADDRESSES (IPv6 Addresses per Netif)|IPv6 Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the number of IPv6 addresses per netif. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_FORWARD|LWIP_IPV6_FORWARD (Forward IPv6 Packets across Netifs)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter enables the forward IPv6 packets across netifs. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_FRAG|LWIP_IPV6_FRAG (Fragment too Big IPv6 Packets)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter allows to fragment outgoing IPv6 packets that are too big. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_REASS|LWIP_IPV6_REASS (Reassemble Incoming IPv6 Fragmented Packets)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter allows to reassemble incoming IPv6 packets that fragmented. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_SEND_ROUTER_SOLICIT|LWIP_IPV6_SEND_ROUTER_SOLICIT (Send Router Solicitation Messages at Network Startup.)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter allows to Send router solicitation messages during network startup. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_AUTOCONFIG|LWIP_IPV6_AUTOCONFIG (Stateless Address Auto-Configuration)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter enables stateless address autoconfiguration as per RFC 4862. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_DUP_DETECT_ATTEMPTS|LWIP_IPV6_DUP_DETECT_ATTEMPTS (Number of Duplicate Address Detection Attempts)|IPv6 Options||0|2147483647|This parameter sets the number of duplicate address detection attempts. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_DHCP6|LWIP_IPV6_DHCP6 (DHCPv6 Stateful Address Auto-Configuration)|IPv6 Options||||This parameter enables DHCPv6 stateful address autoconfiguration. <b>Warning</b>: This feature is not yet supported (see dhcp6.h file: dhcp6 struct is not yet implemented).
LWIP_ICMP6|LWIP_ICMP6 (ICMPv6 Protocol)|IPv6 - ICMP6 Options||||This parameter enables ICMPv6 protocol (mandatory per RFC). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ICMP6_DATASIZE|LWIP_ICMP6_DATASIZE (Original ICMP Packet Size)|IPv6 - ICMP6 Options|Byte(s)|0|2147483647|This parameter set the number of bytes from original packet to send back in ICMPv6 error messages. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ICMP6_HL|LWIP_ICMP6_HL (Hop Limit for ICMPv6 Messages)|IPv6 - ICMP6 Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the default hop limit for ICMPv6 messages. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_IPV6_MLD|LWIP_IPV6_MLD (Multicast Listener Discovery Protocol)|IPv6 - Multicast Listener Discovery Options||||This parameter enables multicast listener discovery protocol. If LWIP_IPV6 is enabled but this setting is disabled, the MAC layer must indiscriminately pass all inbound IPv6 multicast traffic to lwIP. <b>Warning: </b> Enable LWIP_IPV6 when using LWIP_IPV6_MLD, to avoid having compilation warnings on ip6_addr_ismulticast/ip6_current_dest_addr functions. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_NUM_MLD6_GROUP|MEMP_NUM_MLD6_GROUP (Max Number of Joined IPv6 Multicast)|IPv6 - Multicast Listener Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the maximum number of IPv6 multicast groups that can be joined. There must be enough groups so that each netif can join the solicited-node multicast group for each of its local addresses, plus one for MDNS if applicable, plus any number of groups to be joined on UDP sockets. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_QUEUEING|LWIP_ND6_QUEUEING (Queue Outgoing IPv6 Packets)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||||This parameter allows to queue outgoing IPv6 packets while MAC address. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_NUM_ND6_QUEUE|MEMP_NUM_ND6_QUEUE (Max Number of IPv6 Packets to Queue)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the maximum number of IPv6 packets to queue during MAC resolution. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_NUM_NEIGHBORS|LWIP_ND6_NUM_NEIGHBORS (Number of Entries in IPv6 Neighbor Cache)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the number of entries in IPv6 neighbor cache. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_NUM_DESTINATIONS|LWIP_ND6_NUM_DESTINATIONS (Number of Entries in IPv6 Destination Cache)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the number of entries in IPv6 destination cache. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_NUM_PREFIXES|LWIP_ND6_NUM_PREFIXES (Number of Entries in IPv6 On-Link Prefixes Cache)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the number of entries in IPv6 on-link prefixes cache. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_NUM_ROUTERS|LWIP_ND6_NUM_ROUTERS (Number of Entries in IPv6 Default Router Cache)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the number of entries in IPv6 default router cache. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_MAX_MULTICAST_SOLICIT|LWIP_ND6_MAX_MULTICAST_SOLICIT (Max Number of Multicast Solicit Messages to Send)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the max number of multicast solicit messages to send (neighbor solicit and router solicit). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_MAX_UNICAST_SOLICIT|LWIP_ND6_MAX_UNICAST_SOLICIT (Max Number of Unicast Neighbor Solicitation Messages)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter set the max number of unicast neighbor solicitation messages to send during neighbor reachability detection. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_MAX_ANYCAST_DELAY_TIME|LWIP_ND6_MAX_ANYCAST_DELAY_TIME (Max Delay Time for Anycast Messages)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|This parameter is unused: See ND RFC (time in milliseconds). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_MAX_NEIGHBOR_ADVERTISEMENT|LWIP_ND6_MAX_NEIGHBOR_ADVERTISEMENT (Max Number of Neighbor Advertisements)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||0|2147483647|This parameter is unused: See ND RFC. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_REACHABLE_TIME|LWIP_ND6_REACHABLE_TIME (Neigbor Reachable Time)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|This parameter sets the default neighbor reachable time (in milliseconds). May be updated by Router Advertisement message. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_RETRANS_TIMER|LWIP_ND6_RETRANS_TIMER (Retransmission Timer for Solicitation Messages)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|This parameter sets the default retransmission timer for solicitation messages. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_DELAY_FIRST_PROBE_TIME|LWIP_ND6_DELAY_FIRST_PROBE_TIME (Delay Before First Unicast Neighbor Solicitation)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|This parameter sets the Delay before first unicast neighbor solicitation message is sent, during neighbor reachability detection. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_ALLOW_RA_UPDATES|LWIP_ND6_ALLOW_RA_UPDATES (Router Advertisement Messages Update)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||||This parameter allows to allow Router Advertisement messages to update reachable time and retransmission timers, and netif MTU. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_TCP_REACHABILITY_HINTS|LWIP_ND6_TCP_REACHABILITY_HINTS (TCP Neighbor Discovery with Reachability Hints)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||||This parameter allows TCP to provide Neighbor Discovery with reachability hints for connected destinations. This helps avoid sending unicast neighbor solicitation messages. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_ND6_RDNSS_MAX_DNS_SERVERS|LWIP_ND6_RDNSS_MAX_DNS_SERVERS (Use IPv6 Router Advertisement Recursive DNS Server)|IPv6 - Neighboor Discovery Options||||LWIP_ND6_RDNSS_MAX_DNS_SERVERS > 0: Use IPv6 Router Advertisement Recursive DNS Server Option (as per RFC 6106) to copy a defined maximum number of DNS servers to the DNS module. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_HTTPD|LWIP_HTTPD (LwIP HTTPD Support ** CubeMX specific **)|HTTPD Options||||Enable HTTPD requires user specific code not yet generated by STM32CubeMX. This is reserved for advanced users. <b>Warnings</b>: <font color="red">Since HTTPD is only partially supported, some manual actions are required from user: one of user mandatory action is to create the "fsdata_custom.c" file (see HTTPD_USE_CUSTOM_FSDATA advanced parameter description).</font> <b>Note</b>: - This parameter introduced by CubeMX is not part of LwIP stack. - All other HTTPD options are visible as "Advanced Parameters" and only when LWIP_HTTPD is enabled. This httpd supports for a rudimentary server-side-include facility which will replace tags of the form <!--#tag--> in any file whose extension is .shtml, .shtm or .ssi with strings provided by an include handler whose pointer is provided to the module via function http_set_ssi_handler(). Additionally, a simple common gateway interface (CGI) handling mechanism has been added to allow clients to hook functions to particular request URIs. To enable SSI support, define label LWIP_HTTPD_SSI in lwipopts.h. To enable CGI support, define label LWIP_HTTPD_CGI in lwipopts.h. By default, the server assumes that HTTP headers are already present in each file stored in the file system. By defining LWIP_HTTPD_DYNAMIC_HEADERS in lwipopts.h, this behavior can be changed such that the server inserts the headers automatically based on the extension of the file being served. If this mode is used, be careful to ensure that the file system image used does not already contain the header information. File system images without headers can be created using the makefsfile tool with the -h command line option. Notes about valid SSI tags: The following assumptions are made about tags used in SSI markers: 1. No tag may contain '-' or whitespace characters within the tag name. 2. Whitespace is allowed between the tag leadin and the start of the tag name and between the tag name and the leadout string. 3. The maximum tag name length is LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_TAG_NAME_LEN, currently 8 characters. Notes on CGI usage: The simple CGI support offered here works with GET method requests only and can handle up to 16 parameters encoded into the URI. The handler function may not write directly to the HTTP output but must return a filename that the HTTP server will send to the browser as a response to the incoming CGI request. The list of supported file types is quite short, so if makefsdata complains about an unknown extension, make sure to add it (and its doctype) to the 'g_psHTTPHeaders' list.
LWIP_HTTPD_CGI|LWIP_HTTPD_CGI (HTTP CGI Old Style)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to support CGI (old style).
LWIP_HTTPD_CGI_SSI|LWIP_HTTPD_CGI_SSI (HTTP CGI New Style)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to support CGI (new style).
LWIP_HTTPD_SSI|LWIP_HTTPD_SSI (HTTP Server Side Includes)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to support SSI (Server-Side-Includes).
LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_RAW|LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_RAW (HTTP SSI Tag Handler Callback)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to implement an SSI tag handler callback that gets a const char* to the tag (instead of an index into a pre-registered array of known tags).
LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_POST|LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_POST (HTTP POST)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to support HTTP POST.
LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_CGI_PARAMETERS|LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_CGI_PARAMETERS (Max Sent Parameters Number for CGI)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|The maximum number of parameters that the CGI handler can be sent.
LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_MULTIPART|LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_MULTIPART (Server-Side-Includes Multipart)|HTTPD Options||||LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_MULTIPART==1: SSI handler function is called with 2 more arguments indicating a counter for insert string that are too long to be inserted at once: the SSI handler function must then set 'next_tag_part' which will be passed back to it in the next call.
LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_TAG_NAME_LEN|LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_TAG_NAME_LEN (Max Tag Name String Length)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|The maximum length of the string comprising the tag name.
LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_TAG_INSERT_LEN|LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_TAG_INSERT_LEN (Max Tag Inserted String Length)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|The maximum length of string that can be returned to replace any given tag.
LWIP_HTTPD_POST_MANUAL_WND|LWIP_HTTPD_POST_MANUAL_WND (HTTP POST Manual WND)|HTTPD Options||||HTTP POST Manual WND.
HTTPD_SERVER_AGENT|HTTPD_SERVER_AGENT (HTTP Server)|HTTPD Options||||This string is passed in the HTTP header as Server (Example for LwIP 2.0.0).
LWIP_HTTPD_DYNAMIC_HEADERS|LWIP_HTTPD_DYNAMIC_HEADERS (HTTP Dynamic Headers Creation)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 if you want to include code that creates HTTP headers at runtime. Default is off: HTTP headers are then created statically by the makefsdata tool. Static headers mean smaller code size, but the (readonly) fsdata will grow a bit as every file includes the HTTP header.
HTTPD_USE_MEM_POOL|HTTPD_USE_MEM_POOL (HTTP Use Memory Pool)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to use a memp pool for allocating struct http_state instead of the heap.
HTTPD_SERVER_PORT|HTTPD_SERVER_PORT (HTTP Server Port)|HTTPD Options||||The server port for HTTPD to use.
HTTPD_MAX_RETRIES|HTTPD_MAX_RETRIES (HTTP Max Retries)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|Maximum retries before the connection is aborted/closed. - number of times pcb->poll is called -> default is 4*500ms = 2s; - reset when pcb->sent is called.
HTTPD_POLL_INTERVAL|HTTPD_POLL_INTERVAL (HTTP Pool delay)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|The poll delay is X*500ms.
HTTPD_TCP_PRIO|HTTPD_TCP_PRIO (HTTP TCP Priority)|HTTPD Options||1|255|Priority for tcp pcbs created by HTTPD (very low by default). Minimum value is 1 (TCP_PRIO_MIN). Lower priorities get killed first when running out of memory.
LWIP_HTTPD_TIMING|LWIP_HTTPD_TIMING (HTTP Timing)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to enable timing each file sent.
LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_EXTSTATUS|LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_EXTSTATUS (Error Pages Display)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to one to show error pages when parsing a request fails instead of simply closing the connection.
LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_V09|LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_V09 (Drop HTTP 0.9 Support)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 0 to drop support for HTTP/0.9 clients (to save some bytes).
LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_11_KEEPALIVE|LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_11_KEEPALIVE (HTTP 1.1 Persistent Connections)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to enable HTTP/1.1 persistent connections. ATTENTION: If the generated file system includes HTTP headers, these must include the "Connection: keep-alive" header (pass argument "-11" to makefsdata).
LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_REQUESTLIST|LWIP_HTTPD_SUPPORT_REQUESTLIST (HTTP Request from Multiple Packets)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to support HTTP request coming in in multiple packets/pbufs.
LWIP_HTTPD_REQ_QUEUELEN|LWIP_HTTPD_REQ_QUEUELEN (Number of Incoming Rx Pbufs)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|Number of rx pbufs to enqueue to parse an incoming request (up to the first newline).
LWIP_HTTPD_REQ_BUFSIZE|LWIP_HTTPD_REQ_BUFSIZE (Number of TCP Payload in Pbufs)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|Number of (TCP payload-) bytes (in pbufs) to enqueue to parse and incoming request (up to the first double-newline).
LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_REQ_LENGTH|LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_REQ_LENGTH (Max Number of HTTP Request)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|Defines the maximum length of a HTTP request line (up to the first CRLF, copied from pbuf into this a global buffer when pbuf- or packet-queues are received - otherwise the input pbuf is used directly).
LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_REQUEST_URI_LEN|LWIP_HTTPD_MAX_REQUEST_URI_LEN (Max Size Of Static Buffer)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|This is the size of a static buffer used when URIs end with '/'. In this buffer, the directory requested is concatenated with all the configured default file names. Set to 0 to disable checking default filenames on non-root directories.
LWIP_HTTPD_POST_MAX_RESPONSE_URI_LEN|LWIP_HTTPD_POST_MAX_RESPONSE_URI_LEN (Max Filename Length in POST Request Response)|HTTPD Options||0|2147483647|Maximum length of the filename to send as response to a POST request, filled in by the application when a POST is finished.
LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_INCLUDE_TAG|LWIP_HTTPD_SSI_INCLUDE_TAG (Include SSI Tag in HTML Page)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 0 to not send the SSI tag (default is on, so the tag will be sent in the HTML page.
LWIP_HTTPD_ABORT_ON_CLOSE_MEM_ERROR|LWIP_HTTPD_ABORT_ON_CLOSE_MEM_ERROR (Call TCP Abort on TCP Close with Memory Error)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to call tcp_abort when tcp_close fails with memory error. This can be used to prevent consuming all memory in situations where the HTTP server has low priority compared to other communication.
LWIP_HTTPD_KILL_OLD_ON_CONNECTIONS_EXCEEDED|LWIP_HTTPD_KILL_OLD_ON_CONNECTIONS_EXCEEDED (Kill Old Connections)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to kill the oldest connection when running out of memory for 'struct http_state' or 'struct http_ssi_state'. ATTENTION: This puts all connections on a linked list, so may be kind of slow.
LWIP_HTTPD_OMIT_HEADER_FOR_EXTENSIONLESS_URI|LWIP_HTTPD_OMIT_HEADER_FOR_EXTENSIONLESS_URI (Send URIs Without Extension and Headers)|HTTPD Options||||Set this to 1 to send URIs without extension without headers.
HTTPD_LIMIT_SENDING_TO_2MSS||||||By default, the httpd is limited to send 2*pcb->mss to keep resource usage low when http is not an important protocol in the device.
LWIP_HTTPD_CUSTOM_FILES|LWIP_HTTPD_CUSTOM_FILES (HTTP Custom Files)|HTTPD - File System Options||||Set this to 1 and provide the functions: - "int fs_open_custom(struct fs_file *file, const char *name)" Called first for every opened file to allow opening files that are not included in fsdata(_custom).c - "void fs_close_custom(struct fs_file *file)" Called to free resources allocated by fs_open_custom().
LWIP_HTTPD_DYNAMIC_FILE_READ|LWIP_HTTPD_DYNAMIC_FILE_READ (Dynamic File Read)|HTTPD - File System Options||||Set this to 1 to support fs_read() to dynamically read file data. Without this (default=off), only one-block files are supported, and the contents must be ready after fs_open().
LWIP_HTTPD_FILE_STATE|LWIP_HTTPD_FILE_STATE (File State Argument)|HTTPD - File System Options||||Set this to 1 to include an application state argument per file that is opened. This allows to keep a state per connection/file.
HTTPD_PRECALCULATED_CHECKSUM|HTTPD_PRECALCULATED_CHECKSUM (Precompiled Checksum)|HTTPD - File System Options||||HTTPD_PRECALCULATED_CHECKSUM==1: include precompiled checksums for predefined (MSS-sized) chunks of the files to prevent having to calculate the checksums at runtime.
LWIP_HTTPD_FS_ASYNC_READ|LWIP_HTTPD_FS_ASYNC_READ (Asynchronous Read Operation)|HTTPD - File System Options||||LWIP_HTTPD_FS_ASYNC_READ==1: support asynchronous read operations(fs_read_async returns FS_READ_DELAYED and calls a callback when finished).
HTTPD_USE_CUSTOM_FSDATA|HTTPD_USE_CUSTOM_FSDATA (Custom FS Data)|HTTPD - File System Options||||<b>Warnings:</b> - If HTTPD_USE_CUSTOM_FSDATA is enabled : - It requires to have in your generated project folder within Middlewares\Third_Party\LwIP\src\apps\http folder "fsdata_custom.c" instead of "fsdata.c". - Adding "fsdata_custom.c" can be done by renaming existing "fsdata.c" file by "fsdata_custom.c". - If "fsdata.c" has not been copied in your project, it can be taken from the Firmware Repository (Menu Help > Updater Settings...) Software Package folder within Middlewares\Third_Party\LwIP\src\apps\http folder. - Depending on project settings and IDE used, "fsdata_custom.c" or "fsdata.c" can be removed after each code generation. Please take care of this! - If HTTPD_USE_CUSTOM_FSDATA is disabled : - There is no need to rename fsdata.c <b>Limitations</b>: None.
LWIP_SNMP|LWIP_SNMP (LwIP SNMP Agent)|SNMP Options||||<b>Warning: up to user to take care!</b>: Enable SNMP requires user specific code not yet generated by STM32Cube MX. This is reserved for advanced users. <b>Note: </b>All Most SNMP options are visible as "Advanced Parameters" and only when LWIP_SNMP is enabled. This parameter enables the lwIP SNMP agent. UDP must be available for SNMP transport. If you want to use your own SNMP agent, leave this disabled. To integrate MIB2 of an external agent, you need to enable LWIP_MIB2_CALLBACKS and MIB2_STATS. This will give you the callbacks and statistics counters you need to get MIB2 working. Features: - SNMPv2c support. - Low RAM usage - no memory pools, stack only. - MIB2 implementation is separated from SNMP stack. - Support for multiple MIBs (snmp_set_mibs() call) - e.g. for private MIB. - Simple and generic API for MIB implementation. - Comfortable node types and helper functions for scalar arrays and tables. - Counter64, bit and truthvalue datatype support. - Callbacks for SNMP writes e.g. to implement persistency. - Runs on two APIs: RAW and netconn. - Async API is gone - the stack now supports netconn API instead, so blocking operations can be done in MIB calls. SNMP runs in a worker thread when netconn API is used. - Simplified thread sync support for MIBs - useful when MIBs need to access variables shared with other threads where no locking is possible. Used in MIB2 to access lwIP stats from lwIP thread. MIB compiler (code generator): - Written in C#. MIB viewer used Windows Forms. - Developed on Windows with Visual Studio 2010. - Can be compiled and used under Linux with http://www.monodevelop.com/. - Based on a heavily modified version of of SharpSnmpLib (a4bd05c6afb4) (https://sharpsnmplib.codeplex.com/SourceControl/network/forks/Nemo157/MIBParserUpdate). - MIB parser, C file generation framework and LWIP code generation are cleanly separated, which means the code may be useful as a base for code generation of other SNMP agents. Notes: - Stack and MIB compiler were used to implement a Profinet device. Compiled/implemented MIBs: LLDP-MIB, LLDP-EXT-DOT3-MIB, LLDP-EXT-PNO-MIB.
SNMP_USE_NETCONN|SNMP_USE_NETCONN (Use Netconn API)|SNMP Options||||Use netconn API instead of raw API. Makes SNMP agent run in a worker thread, so blocking operations can be done in MIB calls.
SNMP_USE_RAW|SNMP_USE_RAW (Use Raw API)|SNMP Options||||Use raw API. SNMP agent does not run in a worker thread, so blocking operations should not be done in MIB calls. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SNMP_STACK_SIZE|SNMP_STACK_SIZE (Stack Size of SNMP Worker Thread)|SNMP Options|Word(s)|=FREERTOS$context:configMINIMAL_STACK_SIZE|=FREERTOS$context:MAX_SIZE_IN_USE|Stack size of SNMP netconn worker thread.
SNMP_THREAD_PRIO|SNMP_THREAD_PRIO (SNMP Worker Thread Priority)|SNMP Options||0|=FREERTOS$context:configMAX_PRIORITIES-1|SNMP netconn worker thread priority.
SNMP_TRAP_DESTINATIONS|SNMP_TRAP_DESTINATIONS (Number of Trap Destinations)|SNMP Options||1|2147483647|Number of trap destinations. At least one trap destination is required.
SNMP_SAFE_REQUESTS|SNMP_SAFE_REQUESTS (Allow Safe Write Actions)|SNMP Options||||Only allow SNMP write actions that are 'safe' (e.g. disabling netifs is not a safe action and disabled when SNMP_SAFE_REQUESTS = 1). Unsafe requests are disabled by default!.
SNMP_MAX_OCTET_STRING_LEN|SNMP_MAX_OCTET_STRING_LEN (Maximum Length of Strings Used)|SNMP Options||0|2147483647|The maximum length of strings used.
SNMP_MAX_OBJ_ID_LEN|SNMP_MAX_OBJ_ID_LEN (Maximum Number of Object Identifier Sub IDs)|SNMP Options||0|2147483647|The maximum number of Sub ID's inside an object identifier. Indirectly this also limits the maximum depth of SNMP tree.
SNMP_MIN_VALUE_SIZE|SNMP_MIN_VALUE_SIZE (Min Value Size for Basic Types)|SNMP Options||0|2147483647|Size required to store the basic types (8 bytes for counter64). Default value is: 2 * sizeof(u32_t*).
SNMP_MAX_VALUE_SIZE|SNMP_MAX_VALUE_SIZE (Max Value Size)|SNMP Options||0|2147483647|The default value for SNMP_MAX_VALUE_SIZE is: LWIP_MAX(LWIP_MAX((SNMP_MAX_OCTET_STRING_LEN), sizeof(u32_t)*(SNMP_MAX_OBJ_ID_LEN)), SNMP_MIN_VALUE_SIZE).
SNMP_COMMUNITY|SNMP_COMMUNITY (SNMP Read-Access Community)|SNMP Options||||The snmp read-access community. Used for write-access and traps, too unless SNMP_COMMUNITY_WRITE or SNMP_COMMUNITY_TRAP are enabled, respectively.
SNMP_COMMUNITY_WRITE|SNMP_COMMUNITY_WRITE (SNMP Write-Access Community)|SNMP Options||||The snmp write-access community. Set this community to "" in order to disallow any write access.
SNMP_COMMUNITY_TRAP|SNMP_COMMUNITY_TRAP (SNMP Community Sending Traps)|SNMP Options||||The snmp community used for sending traps.
SNMP_MAX_COMMUNITY_STR_LEN|SNMP_MAX_COMMUNITY_STR_LEN (Maximum Community String Length)|SNMP Options||0|2147483647|The maximum length of community string. If community names shall be adjusted at runtime via snmp_set_community() calls, enter here the possible maximum length (+1 for terminating null character).Default value of SNMP_MAX_COMMUNITY_STR_LEN is: LWIP_MAX(LWIP_MAX(sizeof(SNMP_COMMUNITY), sizeof(SNMP_COMMUNITY_WRITE)), sizeof(SNMP_COMMUNITY_TRAP)).
SNMP_LWIP_MIB2|SNMP_LWIP_MIB2 (MIB2 Implementation of LWIP SNMP)|SNMP Options||||Indicates if the MIB2 implementation of LWIP SNMP stack is used.
SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSDESC|SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSDESC (MIB2 SysDesc Value)|SNMP Options||||Value return for sysDesc field of MIB2.
SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSNAME|SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSNAME (MIB2 SysName Value)|SNMP Options||||Value return for sysName field of MIB2. To make sysName field settable, call snmp_mib2_set_sysname() to provide the necessary buffers.
SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSCONTACT|SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSCONTACT (MIB2 SysContact Field Value)|SNMP Options||||Value return for sysContact field of MIB2. To make sysContact field settable, call snmp_mib2_set_syscontact() to provide the necessary buffers.
SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSLOCATION|SNMP_LWIP_MIB2_SYSLOCATION (MIB2 SysLocation Field Value)|SNMP Options||||Value return for sysLocation field of MIB2. To make sysLocation field settable, call snmp_mib2_set_syslocation() to provide the necessary buffers.
SNMP_LWIP_GETBULK_MAX_REPETITIONS|SNMP_LWIP_GETBULK_MAX_REPETITIONS (Max Repetitions in GetBulk Requests)|SNMP Options||0|2147483647|This value is used to limit the repetitions processed in GetBulk requests (value == 0 means no limitation). This may be useful to limit the load for a single request. According to SNMP RFC 1905 it is allowed to not return all requested variables from a GetBulk request if system load would be too high. so the effect is that the client will do more requests to gather all data. For the stack this could be useful in case that SNMP processing is done in TCP/IP thread. In this situation a request with many repetitions could block the thread for a longer time. Setting limit here will keep the stack more responsive.
LWIP_SNMP_V3|LWIP_SNMP_V3 (SNMP Version 3)|SNMP - Version3 Options||||LWIP_SNMP_V3 enables EXPERIMENTAL SNMPv3 support. THIS IS UNDER DEVELOPMENT AND SHOULD NOT BE ENABLED IN PRODUCTS.
LWIP_SNMP_V3_CRYPTO|LWIP_SNMP_V3_CRYPTO (SNMP Version 3 Crypto)|SNMP - Version3 Options||||LWIP_SNMP_V3_CRYPTO enables EXPERIMENTAL SNMPv3 crypto support. THIS IS UNDER DEVELOPMENT AND SHOULD NOT BE ENABLED IN PRODUCTS.
LWIP_SNMP_V3_MBEDTLS|LWIP_SNMP_V3_MBEDTLS (SNMP Version 3 Mbedtls)|SNMP - Version3 Options||||LWIP_SNMP_V3_MBEDTLS enables EXPERIMENTAL SNMPv3 mbedtls support. THIS IS UNDER DEVELOPMENT AND SHOULD NOT BE ENABLED IN PRODUCTS.
LWIP_SNTP|LWIP_SNTP (LWIP SNTP Support ** CubeMX specific **)|SNTP Options||||This parameter provide access to an implementation of a simple "SNTP" client for the lwIP raw API. It is a minimal implementation of SNTPv4 as specified in RFC 4330. For a list of some public NTP servers, see this link : http://support.ntp.org/bin/view/Servers/NTPPoolServers <b>Warnings (up to user to take care!)</b>: - Enable SNTP requires user specific code not yet generated by STM32Cube MX. This is reserved for advanced users - This parameter introduced by CubeMX is not part of LwIP stack - Enable LWIP_TIMERS when using SNTP, to avoid having compilation warnings on sys_timeout/sys_untimeout functions. <b>Note: </b>All other SNTP options are visible as "Advanced Parameters" and only when LWIP_SNTP is enabled.
SNTP_MAX_SERVERS|SNTP_MAX_SERVERS (Maximum Number of SNTP Servers)|SNTP Options||0|2147483647|The maximum number of SNTP servers that can be set.
SNTP_GET_SERVERS_FROM_DHCP|SNTP_GET_SERVERS_FROM_DHCP (Callback Function on DHCP Servers)|SNTP Options||0|2147483647|Set this to 1 to implement the callback function called by dhcp when NTP servers are received.
SNTP_SERVER_DNS|SNTP_SERVER_DNS (DNS Names for SNTP Servers)|SNTP Options||||Set this to 1 to support DNS names (or IP address strings) to set sntp servers. One server address/name can be defined as default if SNTP_SERVER_DNS == 1 (\#define SNTP_SERVER_ADDRESS "pool.ntp.org").
SNTP_PORT|SNTP_PORT (SNTP Server Port)|SNTP Options||0|2147483647|SNTP server port.
SNTP_CHECK_RESPONSE|SNTP_CHECK_RESPONSE (Sanity Check)|SNTP Options||0|2|Sanity check: Define this to - 0 to turn off sanity checks (default; smaller code) - >= 1 to check address and port of the response packet to ensure the response comes from the server we sent the request to. - >= 2 to check returned Originate Timestamp against Transmit Timestamp sent to the server (to ensure response to older request). - >= 3 @todo: discard reply if any of the LI, Stratum, or Transmit Timestamp fields is 0 or the Mode field is not 4 (unicast) or 5 (broadcast). - >= 4 @todo: to check that the Root Delay and Root Dispersion fields are each greater than or equal to 0 and less than infinity, where infinity is currently a cozy number like one second. This check avoids using a server whose synchronization source has expired for a very long time.
SNTP_STARTUP_DELAY|SNTP_STARTUP_DELAY (Delay to Prevent Load Peaks)|SNTP Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|=5*60*1000|According to the RFC, this shall be a random delay between 1 and 5 minutes (in milliseconds) to prevent load peaks. This can be defined to a random generation function, which must return the delay in milliseconds as u32_t. Turned off by default.
SNTP_STARTUP_DELAY_FUNC|SNTP_STARTUP_DELAY_FUNC (Startup Delay Function)|SNTP Options||0|2147483647|If you want the startup delay to be a function, define this to a function (including the brackets) and define SNTP_STARTUP_DELAY to 1.
SNTP_RECV_TIMEOUT|SNTP_RECV_TIMEOUT (SNTP Receive Timeout)|SNTP Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|SNTP receive timeout - in milliseconds Also used as retry timeout - this shouldn't be too low. Default is 3 seconds.
SNTP_UPDATE_DELAY|SNTP_UPDATE_DELAY (SNTP Update Delay)|SNTP Options|Millisecond(ms)|=15*1000|2147483647|SNTP update delay - in milliseconds Default is 1 hour. Must not be beolw 15 seconds by specification (i.e. 15000).
SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT|SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT (SNTP Default Retry Timeout)|SNTP Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|Default retry timeout (in milliseconds) if the response received is invalid. This is doubled with each retry until SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT_MAX is reached.
SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT_MAX|SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT_MAX (Maximum SNTP Retry Timeout)|SNTP Options|Millisecond(ms)|0|2147483647|Maximum retry timeout (in milliseconds).
SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT_EXP|SNTP_RETRY_TIMEOUT_EXP (Retry Timeout Increase)|SNTP Options||||Increase retry timeout with every retry sent Default is on to conform to RFC.
LWIP_MDNS|LWIP_MDNS (Multicast DNS Support ** CubeMX specific **)|MDNS Options||||This parameter provides access to an implementation of a MDNS responder. The following RFC have been followed: - RFC 6762 (Multicast DNS) - RFC 6763 (DNS-Based Service Discovery). <b>Note: </b>All Other MDNS options are visible as "Advanced Parameters" and only when LWIP_MDNS is enabled. <b>Warnings</b>: - This parameter introduced by CubeMX is not part of LwIP stack - Enable MDNS requires user specific code not yet generated by STM32Cube MX, this is reserved for advanced users. Things left to implement: - Probing/conflict resolution - Sending goodbye messages (zero ttl) - shutdown, DHCP lease about to expire, DHCP turned off... - Checking that source address of unicast requests are on the same network - Limiting multicast responses to 1 per second per resource record - Fragmenting replies if required - Subscribe to netif address/link change events and act on them (currently needs to be done manually) - Handling multi-packet known answers - Individual known answer detection for all local IPv6 addresses - Dynamic size of outgoing packet
LWIP_MDNS_RESPONDER|LWIP_MDNS_RESPONDER (Multicast DNS Responder)|MDNS Options||||Set this to 1 to turn on multicast DNS module. UDP must be available for MDNS transport. IGMP is needed for IPv4 multicast.
MDNS_MAX_SERVICES|MDNS_MAX_SERVICES (Maximum Number of Netif Services)|MDNS Options||0|2147483647|Use this parameter to set the maximum number of services per netif.
LWIP_TFTP|LWIP_TFTP (TFTP Support ** CubeMX specific **)|TFTP Options||||This parameter provide access to an implementation of a simple TFTP server for the lwIP raw API. <b>Warnings</b>: - This parameter introduced by CubeMX is not part of LwIP stack. - Enable TFTP requires user specific code not yet generated by STM32Cube MX. This is reserved for advanced users. - Enable LWIP_TIMERS when using TFTP, to avoid having compilation warnings on sys_timeout/sys_untimeout functions.<b>Note: </b>All Other TFTP options are visible as "Advanced Parameters" and only when LWIP_TFTP is enabled.
TFTP_PORT|TFTP_PORT (TFTP Server Port)|TFTP Options||0|2147483647|This option set the server port number.
TFTP_TIMEOUT_MSECS|TFTP_TIMEOUT_MSECS (TFTP Timeout)|TFTP Options||0|2147483647|This option set TFTP timeout.
TFTP_MAX_RETRIES|TFTP_MAX_RETRIES (TFTP Maximum Retries Number)|TFTP Options||0|2147483647|This option set the maximum number of retries when a file is read from server.
TFTP_TIMER_MSECS|TFTP_TIMER_MSECS (TFTP Timer Cyclic Interval)|TFTP Options||0|2147483647|This option set the timer cyclic interval.
TFTP_MAX_FILENAME_LEN|TFTP_MAX_FILENAME_LEN (TFTP Filename Maximum Length)|TFTP Options||0|2147483647|This option set the maximum length of TFTP filename.
TFTP_MAX_MODE_LEN|TFTP_MAX_MODE_LEN (TFTP Mode Maximum Length)|TFTP Options||0|2147483647|This option set the maximum length of TFTP mode.
LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS|LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS (TCP Sanity Checks)|Sanity Checks||||Activate some checks on TCP parameters. <b>TCP Checks</b>: If LWIP_DISABLE_TCP_SANITY_CHECKS is disabled (=0), check is performed on MEMP_NUM_TCP_SEG, TCP_SND_BUF, TCP_SND_QUEUELEN, TCP_SNDLOWAT, TCP_SNDQUEUELOWAT, PBUF_POOL_BUFSIZE and TCP_WND parameters.
LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS|LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS (MEMP Sanity Checks)|Sanity Checks||||Activate some checks on MEMP_NUM_NETCONN parameter. <b>MEMP Checks</b>: If LWIP_DISABLE_MEMP_SANITY_CHECKS is disabled (=0), check is performed on MEMP_NUM_NETCONN.
LWIP_PERF|LWIP_PERF (Performace Testing for LwIP)|Performance Options||||This parameter enables performance testing for lwIP (if enabled, arch/perf.h is included). <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_STATS|LWIP_STATS (Statictics Collection)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable statistics collection in lwip_stats. <b>Note: </b>All other statistics options are visible as "Advanced Parameters". <b>Dependency</b>: This global option is linked to all other Statistics options. LWIP_STATS must be enabled if SNMP_LWIP_MIB2 is enabled.
LWIP_STATS_DISPLAY|LWIP_STATS_DISPLAY (Compile in Statictics Ouput Functions)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Compile in the statistics output functions.
LINK_STATS|LINK_STATS (Link Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable link stats.
ETHARP_STATS|ETHARP_STATS (Ethernet ARP Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable etharp stats.
IP_STATS|IP_STATS (IP Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable IP stats.
IPFRAG_STATS|IPFRAG_STATS (IP Fragmentation Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable IP fragmentation stats. Default is on if using either fragmentation or reassembly.
ICMP_STATS|ICMP_STATS (ICMP Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable ICMP stats.
IGMP_STATS|IGMP_STATS (IGMP Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable IGMP stats.
UDP_STATS|UDP_STATS (UDP Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable UDP stats.
TCP_STATS|TCP_STATS (TCP Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable TCP stats. Default is on if TCP enabled, otherwise off.
MEM_STATS|MEM_STATS (Memory Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable mem.c stats.
MEMP_STATS|MEMP_STATS (Memory Pool Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable memp.c pool stats.
SYS_STATS|SYS_STATS (System Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable system stats (sem and mbox counts, etc).
IP6_STATS|IP6_STATS (IPv6 Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable IPv6 stats.
ICMP6_STATS|ICMP6_STATS (ICMP for IPv6 Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable ICMP for IPv6 stats.
IP6_FRAG_STATS|IP6_FRAG_STATS (IPv6 Fragmentation Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable IPv6 fragmentation stats.
MLD6_STATS|MLD6_STATS (MLD for IPv6 Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable MLD for IPv6 stats.
ND6_STATS|ND6_STATS (Neighbor Discovery for IPv6 Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable Neighbor discovery for IPv6 stats.
MIB2_STATS|MIB2_STATS (SNMP MIB2 Statistics)|Debug - Statistics Options||||Enable or Disable SNMP MIB2 statistics.
CHECKSUM_BY_HARDWARE|CHECKSUM_BY_HARDWARE (Hardware Checksum ** CubeMX specific **)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Enabling Hardware Checksum will disable software checksum parameters and allow to compute and verify the IP, UDP, TCP and ICMP checksums by Hardware. Since CubeMx 6.0.0 release, It is enabled like in LwIP Cube FW applications. <b>Warning</b>: This parameter introduced by CubeMX is not part of LwIP stack.
LWIP_CHECKSUM_CTRL_PER_NETIF|LWIP_CHECKSUM_CTRL_PER_NETIF (Generate/Check Checksum per Netif)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Checksum generation/check can be enabled/disabled per netif. ATTENTION: if enabled, the CHECKSUM_GEN_* and CHECKSUM_CHECK_* defines must be enabled!
CHECKSUM_GEN_IP|CHECKSUM_GEN_IP (Generate Software Checksum for Outgoing IP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Generate checksums in software for outgoing IP packets.
CHECKSUM_GEN_UDP|CHECKSUM_GEN_UDP (Generate Software Checksum for Outgoing UDP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Generate checksums in software for outgoing UDP packets.
CHECKSUM_GEN_TCP|CHECKSUM_GEN_TCP (Generate Software Checksum for Outgoing TCP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Generate checksums in software for outgoing TCP packets.
CHECKSUM_GEN_ICMP|CHECKSUM_GEN_ICMP (Generate Software Checksum for Outgoing ICMP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Generate checksums in software for outgoing ICMP packets.
CHECKSUM_GEN_ICMP6|CHECKSUM_GEN_ICMP6 (Generate Software Checksum for Outgoing ICMP6 Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Generate checksums in software for outgoing ICMP6 packets.
CHECKSUM_CHECK_IP|CHECKSUM_CHECK_IP (Generate Software Checksum for Incoming IP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Check checksums in software for Incoming IP packets.
CHECKSUM_CHECK_UDP|CHECKSUM_CHECK_UDP (Generate Software Checksum for Incoming UDP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Check checksums in software for Incoming UDP packets.
CHECKSUM_CHECK_TCP|CHECKSUM_CHECK_TCP (Generate Software Checksum for Incoming TCP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Check checksums in software for Incoming TCP packets.
CHECKSUM_CHECK_ICMP|CHECKSUM_CHECK_ICMP (Generate Software Checksum for Incoming ICMP Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Check checksums in software for Incoming ICMP packets.
CHECKSUM_CHECK_ICMP6|CHECKSUM_CHECK_ICMP6 (Generate Software Checksum for Incoming ICMP6 Packets)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Check checksums in software for Incoming ICMP6 packets.
LWIP_CHECKSUM_ON_COPY|LWIP_CHECKSUM_ON_COPY (Calculate Checksum on Copy to Pbufs)|Infrastructure - Checksum Options||||Calculate checksum when copying data from application buffers to pbufs. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DBG_ON|LWIP_DBG_ON (Enable Debug Message)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||
LWIP_DBG_OFF|LWIP_DBG_OFF (Disable Debug Message)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||
LWIP_DBG_MIN_LEVEL|LWIP_DBG_MIN_LEVEL (Minimum Level)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Lower two bits indicate debug level: - 0: all - 1: warning - 2: serious - 3: severe - 4: Mask. <b>Note: </b>All other debugging options are visible as "Advanced Parameters". <b>Dependency</b>: None.
LWIP_DBG_TYPES_ON|LWIP_DBG_TYPES_ON (Only certain Debug Message Types)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Mask that can be used to globally enable/disable debug messages of certain types. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ETHARP_DEBUG|ETHARP_DEBUG (Debug in Ethernet ARP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in etharp.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
NETIF_DEBUG|NETIF_DEBUG (Debug in NETIF)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in netif.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PBUF_DEBUG|PBUF_DEBUG (Debug in Pbuf)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in pbuf.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
API_LIB_DEBUG|API_LIB_DEBUG (Debug in API Library)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in api_lib.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
API_MSG_DEBUG|API_MSG_DEBUG (Debug in API Message)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in api_msg.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SOCKETS_DEBUG|SOCKETS_DEBUG (Debug in Sockets)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in sockets.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
ICMP_DEBUG|ICMP_DEBUG (Debug in ICMP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in icmp.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
IGMP_DEBUG|IGMP_DEBUG (Debug in IGMP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in igmp.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
INET_DEBUG|INET_DEBUG (Debug in INET)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in inet.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
IP_DEBUG|IP_DEBUG (Debug in IP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for IP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
IP_REASS_DEBUG|IP_REASS_DEBUG (Debug in IP Reassembly)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in ip_frag.c for both frag and reass. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
RAW_DEBUG|RAW_DEBUG (Debug in RAW API)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in raw.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEM_DEBUG|MEM_DEBUG (Debug in Memory)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in mem.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MEMP_DEBUG|MEMP_DEBUG (Debug in Memory Pool)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in memp.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SYS_DEBUG|SYS_DEBUG (Debug in System)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in sys.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TIMERS_DEBUG|TIMERS_DEBUG (Debug in Timers)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in timers.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_DEBUG|TCP_DEBUG (Debug in TCP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for TCP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_INPUT_DEBUG|TCP_INPUT_DEBUG (Debug in TCP Incoming)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in tcp_in.c for incoming debug. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_FR_DEBUG|TCP_FR_DEBUG (Debug in TCP Incoming for Fast Retransmit)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in tcp_in.c for fast retransmit. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_RTO_DEBUG|TCP_RTO_DEBUG (Debug in TCP for Retransmit)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in TCP for retransmit. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_CWND_DEBUG|TCP_CWND_DEBUG (Debug in TCP Congestion Window)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for TCP congestion window. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_WND_DEBUG|TCP_WND_DEBUG (Debug in TCP for Window Updating)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in tcp_in.c for window updating. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_OUTPUT_DEBUG|TCP_OUTPUT_DEBUG (Debug in TCP Output Functions)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in tcp_out.c output functions. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_RST_DEBUG|TCP_RST_DEBUG (Debug in TCP with RST Message)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for TCP with the RST message. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCP_QLEN_DEBUG|TCP_QLEN_DEBUG (Debug in TCP Queue Lengths)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for TCP queue lengths. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
UDP_DEBUG|UDP_DEBUG (Debug in UDP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in UDP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TCPIP_DEBUG|TCPIP_DEBUG (Debug in TCPIP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in tcpip.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SLIP_DEBUG|SLIP_DEBUG (Debug in SLIPIF)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in slipif.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DHCP_DEBUG|DHCP_DEBUG (Debug in DHCP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in dhcp.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
AUTOIP_DEBUG|AUTOIP_DEBUG (Debug in AUTOIP)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging in autoip.c. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
DNS_DEBUG|DNS_DEBUG (Debug for DNS)|LwIP Main Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for DNS. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
PPP_DEBUG|PPP_DEBUG (Debug in PPP)|LwIP - PPP Debugging Option||||Enable debugging for PPP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
IP6_DEBUG|IP6_DEBUG (Debug for IPv6)|LwIP - IPv6 Debugging Option||||Enable debugging for SNMP IPv6. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
HTTPD_DEBUG|HTTPD_DEBUG (HTTP Debug)|LwIP - HTTPD Debugging Options||||Set this to 1 if you want to activate HTTP debug. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
HTTPD_DEBUG_TIMING|HTTPD_DEBUG_TIMING (HTTP Debug Timing)|LwIP - HTTPD Debugging Options||||Set this to 1 if you want to activate HTTP debug timing. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SNMP_DEBUG|SNMP_DEBUG (SNMP Debug)|LwIP - SNMP Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for SNMP messages. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SNMP_MIB_DEBUG|SNMP_MIB_DEBUG (SNMP Debug for MIBs)|LwIP - SNMP Debugging Options||||Enable debugging for SNMP MIBs. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
SNTP_DEBUG|SNTP_DEBUG (SNTP Debug)|LwIP - SNTP Debugging Option||||Enable debugging for SNTP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
MDNS_DEBUG|MDNS_DEBUG (Debug for MDNS)|LwIP - MDNS Debugging Option||||Enable debugging for MDNS. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
TFTP_DEBUG|TFTP_DEBUG (Debug for TFTP)|LwIP - TFTP Debugging Option||||Enable debugging for TFTP. <b>Dependency</b>: None.
